// StdlibSource — what a compile knows about the STDLIB SOURCE it loaded, as opposed to the user's own
// code. Two facts, derived once per compile from one walk of the merged Maxon module:
//
//   1. WHICH STDLIB FUNCTIONS THIS PROGRAM CANNOT REACH — code dead-function elimination will drop
//      before the back end, so no pre-elimination pass may let it decide anything about the program.
//   2. WHICH STDLIB FUNCTIONS REACH A HOST ENTRY THE TARGET LACKS, and which entry each one reaches —
//      the fact a user's own call site is refused with, so a target rejection names the stdlib function
//      THEY wrote instead of a line inside `stdlib/`.
//
// ⭐ **NOTHING HERE DEPENDS ON *WHICH* STDLIB FILES ARE LOADED — only on which functions came from
// stdlib.** The compiler loads a growing subset of `stdlib/` today and all of it eventually; both facts above get
// MORE important in that end state, not less, because a program built on `Map`, `String` and `FilePath`
// crosses into stdlib on nearly every line, and a diagnostic positioned inside one of them is a
// diagnostic about a file the user never opened.
//
// So the question asked throughout is **"is this function STDLIB source or USER source?"**, answered from
// the function's own source path (`isLibraryFunction`) — never from a registry of loaded modules. Grow the
// set of loaded files to every file under `stdlib/` and every function in this file still answers
// correctly, over a larger set, with no edit.

// ⭐ **WHICH FILES ARE THE LIBRARY'S IS ESTABLISHED BY CONSTRUCTION, NOT BY RE-TESTING PATHS.** The
// loader enumerates BOTH library roots — `project.stdlibDir` and its sibling `project.runtimeDir` — and
// records every file it parsed from either in `project.librarySourcePaths`, so "this file came out of a
// library root" is already known of exactly those files, and of no others. That record is what the facts
// below are derived from, and the two roots are one population here because they are one population to
// every consumer: code the compile loads that the program's author did not write.
//
// ⚠ **THE ALTERNATIVE IS EXPENSIVE.** Re-deriving it by resolving each of the program's source paths
// against the working directory and asking `FilePath.isInside` costs on the order of a HUNDRED
// allocations a question — `resolve` and `isInside` build a String per path component, and on Windows
// `canonicalForCompare` clones and lower-cases both sides — so it puts a term into every compile that
// grows with the number of files the user wrote. Asking it per FUNCTION instead of per file is worse
// again. `isLibraryFunction` answers the per-function form from a set built once.
//
// ⭐ **IS THIS FUNCTION LIBRARY SOURCE?** A `ByteArray` hash against that set. This is the question every
// pass asks, and it stays true as the library grows from one module to all of it — nothing here depends
// on WHICH files those are, only on their having come from a library root.
export function isLibraryFunction(project Project, func IrFunction) returns bool
	return project.libraryFacts.libraryFunctions.contains(func.name)
end 'isLibraryFunction'

// The directory name walked for, and a generous bound on how far up the tree to look. A checkout is
// shallow (`<root>/maxon-bin/.maxon/maxon.exe` is three levels under the `stdlib/` that sits at
// `<root>`), so 64 is far more than enough while still terminating on a pathological path rather than
// looping forever.
//
// ⚠ **EXPORTED BECAUSE IT IS ALSO THE STDLIB'S MODULE DIRECTORY** — `Queries.namespaceAnchorFor`
// anchors a stdlib file at this directory's PARENT, so every stdlib file's module directory is this
// name or a descendant of it. Its readers (`ProgramSignatures.namespaceKeyOf`,
// `Queries.reservedNamespaceHeads` among them) read this rather than a second `"stdlib"` literal so
// a rename cannot leave them disagreeing about where the library layer is.
export let StdlibDirName = "stdlib"
let MaxStdlibSearchDepth = 64

// The second library root, `runtime/` — the language runtime, loaded by every compile exactly as
// `stdlib/` is.
export let RuntimeDirName = "runtime"

// Anchored on the executable, not `Directory.currentPath()`: the compiler may run from any
// directory (the spec runner compiles in a throwaway temp dir), while its binary always lives inside
// the checkout. A missing `stdlib/` must throw: every compile loads stdlib source, and a skip would
// surface as an unexplained E3004 on the first stdlib call.
export function locateStdlibDir() returns FilePath throws CompileError
	let exePath = try Process.executablePath() otherwise throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound
	let exeDir = try exePath.parent() otherwise throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound
	let exeIdentity = try projectIdentityPathOf(exeDir) otherwise (cause) 'unidentifiable'
		throw unidentifiablePathFailure(cause)
	end 'unidentifiable'

	return try stdlibDirAbove(exeIdentity)
end 'locateStdlibDir'

// ⭐⭐ **`runtime/` IS DERIVED FROM `stdlib/`'s LOCATION AND NEVER WALKED FOR ON ITS OWN.** An
// independent walk could stop at a `runtime/` from a different tree than the `stdlib/` the same walk
// found — a released library paired with a tree's runtime, or the reverse — and a compile built from
// two halves of two installs is wrong in a way nothing downstream could detect. Deriving makes that
// pairing UNREPRESENTABLE, so there is no error code for it and no case to write.
//
// ⚠ It takes the `stdlib/` rather than locating one, because every caller is already holding the
// directory whose sibling it wants — `Compiler.runtimeDirFor` the compile's own (which may be a STAGED
// copy), `RunCache.libraryInputs` the one it just walked. A no-argument twin would re-run the walk-up
// and, handed a staged stdlib, silently answer about the checkout instead.
export function runtimeDirBeside(stdlibDir FilePath) returns FilePath throws CompileError
	let checkoutRoot = try stdlibDir.parent() otherwise throw CompileError.runtimeNotFound

	return checkoutRoot.join("{RuntimeDirName}")
end 'runtimeDirBeside'

export let ScriptlibDirName = "scriptlib"

export function scriptlibDirBeside(stdlibDir FilePath) returns FilePath
	let checkoutRoot = try stdlibDir.parent() otherwise panic("scriptlibDirBeside: '{stdlibDir.toString()}' has no parent — it is the absolute stdlib directory a walk found, and a directory holds it")

	return checkoutRoot.join("{ScriptlibDirName}")
end 'scriptlibDirBeside'

export union ScriptlibLoad
	besideStdlib

	// For harnesses and gates: a developer's own `scriptlib/` must never reach what they compile.
	omitted

	checking(directory FilePath)

	export function tierBeside(stdlibDir FilePath) returns ScriptlibTier
		return match self 'load'
			besideStdlib gives ScriptlibTier.at(scriptlibDirBeside(stdlibDir), bodies: LibraryBodies.onDemand)
			omitted gives ScriptlibTier.absent
			checking(directory) gives ScriptlibTier.at(directory.resolve(Directory.currentPath()), bodies: LibraryBodies.every)
		end 'load'
	end 'tierBeside'
end 'ScriptlibLoad'

export enum LibraryBodies
	onDemand
	every
end 'LibraryBodies'

export union ScriptlibTier
	absent
	at(root FilePath, bodies LibraryBodies)

	export function packagesRoot() returns FilePath throws AbsentError
		match self 'tier'
			absent then throw AbsentError.absent
			at(root, _) then return root
		end 'tier'
	end 'packagesRoot'

	export function physical() returns ScriptlibTier throws CompileError
		return match self 'tier'
			absent gives ScriptlibTier.absent
			at(root, bodies) gives ScriptlibTier.at(try identityOfPath(root), bodies: bodies)
		end 'tier'
	end 'physical'

	export function holds(resolvedPath FilePath) returns bool
		let root = try self.packagesRoot() otherwise return false

		return resolvedPath.isInside(root)
	end 'holds'

	export function buildsEveryBody() returns bool
		return match self 'tier'
			absent gives false
			at(_, bodies) gives bodies == LibraryBodies.every
		end 'tier'
	end 'buildsEveryBody'
end 'ScriptlibTier'

// The same walk, from a directory the CALLER names — which is a different question from the one above
// and has one other asker: `TreeLock` needs the checkout containing the path a command WRITES INTO
// (the project directory, the spec directory, the scale corpus), not the one the compiler's own
// sources came from. Those two answers are the same file in every ordinary invocation and are not
// required to be: the walk is what both of them mean by "the checkout", so it is written once.
//
// It THROWS rather than answering "none" for `locateStdlibDir`'s reason there; `TreeLock` reads the
// throw as "no checkout above this path", which is a real and ordinary answer for a program compiled
// in a temp directory.
export function stdlibDirAbove(start FilePath) returns FilePath throws CompileError
	var dir = start

	var depth = 0 as Count

	while depth < MaxStdlibSearchDepth 'search'
		let candidate = dir.join("{StdlibDirName}")

		if Directory.exists(candidate) 'found'
			return candidate
		end 'found'

		// `parent()` throws at the filesystem root — the correct place for the search to give up.
		dir = try dir.parent() otherwise throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound
		depth = depth + 1
	end 'search'

	throw CompileError.stdlibNotFound
end 'stdlibDirAbove'

// A set of function names, for O(1) membership. Used for both "unreachable from `main`" and "declared in
// stdlib source", exactly like `DestructorSet`.
export typealias FunctionNameSet = Set with ByteArray

// ⭐⭐ **THE BODY DEMAND IS PER FILE AND HAS TWO STATES — all of this file's bodies, or none of them.**
// A user file always builds. A stdlib file builds once ANY function it declares is reached, and then it
// builds ALL of them.
//
// ⚠ **PER FILE RATHER THAN PER FUNCTION, AND BOTH REASONS ARE LOAD-BEARING:**
//
//   • **CORRECTNESS.** A per-function demand has to be asked in the parser, mid-declaration, of a name —
//     and there are TWO spellings of a function's name there. `sourceName` is what the source wrote
//     (`Math.exp`); a free function whose bare name a second directory also declares is registered under
//     a minted `<dir>~name` (`freeFunctionRegistrationName`), and it is the MINTED one that reaches
//     `IrFunction.name` and therefore the reachability walk's answer. Asking with the wrong one skips a
//     body the program calls, and the call then lands on the bare `retVoid`
//     `skipUnreachedFunctionBody` left — a SILENT WRONG ANSWER. A per-FILE demand never asks about a name
//     at all.
//   • **COST.** The demand is discovered round by round, so a per-function demand re-parses a file once per
//     function of it that is reached — and, because a memo key built from the whole-program demand moves
//     for EVERY file whenever ANY name is added, it re-parses every listed file each round — a cost that
//     lands on the very programs this exists to speed up.
//
// The cost of the coarser unit is building the unreached bodies of a file that is already being used, and
// `DeadFunctionElimination` drops those like any other unreached body.
// (There is no typealias for it: `typealias X = bool` is not legal Maxon, and a one-word alias for a
// primitive would be a name to keep in step rather than a fact to state. The header above is the fact.)

// A stdlib function name -> the host entry this compile's target lacks that its body reaches, directly
// or through other stdlib functions. Membership IS "this function cannot be lowered for the target", so
// the map is its own mark column and there is no second set to keep in step with it.
export typealias StdlibSubstrateEntryMap = Map with (ByteArray, immutable ByteArray)

// Stdlib function name -> its DENSE node index in the substrate fixpoint's call graph. A distinct alias
// from `FuncIndexByName`, whose values are merged-MODULE function indices: these index the far
// smaller stdlib-only key space `buildCsr` is handed.
typealias StdlibNodeByName = Map with (ByteArray, CsrIndex)

// The service types whose loop handlers the walk has credited, so a type spawned at many sites pays for its
// handler roster once rather than once per `spawn`.
typealias CreditedServiceSet = Set with ByteArray
typealias ConstrainedOwnerSet = Set with ByteArray

typealias TupleNameSet = Set with TypeNameId

enum MentionKind
	genericInstanceType
	namedType
	scalar
end 'MentionKind'

function mentionKindOf(tag ValueTypeTag) returns MentionKind
	return match tag 'kind'
		genericInstance gives MentionKind.genericInstanceType
		named or
		structRef gives MentionKind.namedType
		boolean or
		integer or
		float or
		exitCode or
		unresolved or
		void or
		string or
		function or
		typeParameter or
		character or
		interfaceRef or
		cstring gives MentionKind.scalar
	end 'kind'
end 'mentionKindOf'

type TypeMentions
	export var instances as GenericInstanceIdArray
	export var typeIds as TypeNameIdArray
	export var argumentTypeNames as NameRoster
	let instanceSeen as FixedBoolArray
	var tuplesSeen as TupleNameSet

	static function over(signatures ProgramSignatures) returns Self
		return Self{instances: GenericInstanceIdArray.create(0), typeIds: TypeNameIdArray.create(0), argumentTypeNames: NameRoster.create(0), instanceSeen: FixedBoolArray.create(signatures.genericInstanceCount() as Count), tuplesSeen: TupleNameSet.create()}
	end 'over'

	function reset()
		for instance in self.instances 'eachPreviousInstance'
			try self.instanceSeen.set(instance, value: false) otherwise panic("TypeMentions.reset: instanceSeen.set OOB at instance {instance}")
		end 'eachPreviousInstance'

		self.instances.clear()
		self.typeIds.clear()
		self.argumentTypeNames.clear()

		if self.tuplesSeen.count() > 0 'someTupleWasWalked'
			self.tuplesSeen = TupleNameSet.create()
		end 'someTupleWasWalked'
	end 'reset'

	static function none() returns Self
		return Self{instances: GenericInstanceIdArray.create(0), typeIds: TypeNameIdArray.create(0), argumentTypeNames: NameRoster.create(0), instanceSeen: FixedBoolArray.create(1), tuplesSeen: TupleNameSet.create()}
	end 'none'

	function gather(func IrFunction, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, skipping LibraryCallResults)
		self.reset()

		for position in 0 upto func.valueTags.count() 'eachValue'
			if skipping.holds(position as ValueId) 'aBlamedCallResult'
				continue
			end 'aBlamedCallResult'

			self.noteValue(func, value: position as ValueId, project: project, signatures: signatures)
		end 'eachValue'

		for paramType in func.maxonParamTypes 'eachParam'
			self.note(maxonTypeTag(paramType), nameId: typeNameIdOf(paramType), project: project, signatures: signatures)
		end 'eachParam'

		match func.maxonReturnType 'returnPosition'
			void then break
			value(inner) then self.note(maxonTypeTag(inner), nameId: typeNameIdOf(inner), project: project, signatures: signatures)
		end 'returnPosition'
	end 'gather'

	function gatherValue(func IrFunction, value ValueId, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures)
		self.reset()
		self.noteValue(func, value: value, project: project, signatures: signatures)
	end 'gatherValue'

	function noteValue(func IrFunction, value ValueId, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures)
		let tag = try func.valueTags.get(value) otherwise panic("TypeMentions.noteValue: valueTags.get OOB at v{value} in '{String.from(func.name)}'")
		let nameId = try func.valueNameIds.get(value) otherwise panic("TypeMentions.noteValue: valueNameIds.get OOB at v{value} in '{String.from(func.name)}'")

		self.note(tag, nameId: nameId, project: project, signatures: signatures)
	end 'noteValue'

	function note(tag ValueTypeTag, nameId TypeNameId, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures)
		match mentionKindOf(tag) 'kind'
			genericInstanceType then self.noteInstance(nameId as GenericInstanceId, signatures: signatures)
			namedType then self.noteNamedType(nameId, project: project, signatures: signatures)
			scalar then break
		end 'kind'
	end 'note'

	function noteWithin(argument MaxonType, signatures ProgramSignatures)
		match mentionKindOf(maxonTypeTag(argument)) 'kind'
			genericInstanceType then self.noteInstance(genericInstanceIdOf(argument), signatures: signatures)
			namedType then self.noteTupleElements(argument, signatures: signatures)
			scalar then break
		end 'kind'
	end 'noteWithin'

	function noteInstance(instance GenericInstanceId, signatures ProgramSignatures)
		let known = try self.instanceSeen.get(instance) otherwise panic("TypeMentions.noteInstance: instanceSeen.get OOB at instance {instance}")

		if known 'alreadyNoted'
			return
		end 'alreadyNoted'

		try self.instanceSeen.set(instance, value: true) otherwise panic("TypeMentions.noteInstance: instanceSeen.set OOB at instance {instance}")
		self.instances.push(instance)

		for position in 0 upto signatures.genericInstanceArgCount(instance) 'eachArgument'
			let argument = signatures.instanceArgType(instance, position: position)

			if mentionKindOf(maxonTypeTag(argument)) == MentionKind.namedType 'aNamedArgument'
				self.argumentTypeNames.push(freeze signatures.conformerTypeArgName(instance, position: position).toByteArray())
			end 'aNamedArgument'

			self.noteWithin(argument, signatures: signatures)
		end 'eachArgument'
	end 'noteInstance'

	function noteNamedType(nameId TypeNameId, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures)
		self.typeIds.push(nameId)

		if not internedNameIsATupleTypeName(project.typeNames, id: nameId) or self.tuplesSeen.contains(nameId) 'nothingMoreToWalk'
			return
		end 'nothingMoreToWalk'

		self.tuplesSeen.insert(nameId)

		let name = try project.typeNames.nameOf(nameId) otherwise panic("TypeMentions.noteNamedType: type-name id {nameId} is not in the project's interner, which every value type of a merged module was folded into")
		let signatureId = try signatures.typeNameTable().idOf(name) otherwise return

		self.noteTupleElements(MaxonType.structRef(signatureId), signatures: signatures)
	end 'noteNamedType'

	function noteTupleElements(tuple MaxonType, signatures ProgramSignatures)
		let elements = try signatures.tupleElementTypesOf(tuple) otherwise return

		for element in elements 'eachTupleElement'
			if mentionKindOf(maxonTypeTag(element)) == MentionKind.namedType 'aNamedElement'
				self.argumentTypeNames.push(freeze signatures.conformerNameOfType(element, idsFrom: signatures.typeNameTable(), readerFilePath: CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath).toByteArray())
			end 'aNamedElement'

			self.noteWithin(element, signatures: signatures)
		end 'eachTupleElement'
	end 'noteTupleElements'
end 'TypeMentions'

typealias ConformerNameById = Map with (TypeNameId, immutable ByteArray)
typealias InterfacesByEnum = Map with (ByteArray, NameRoster)
typealias ImplsByName = Map with (ByteArray, CreditedImpls)
typealias ImplsByInstance = Map with (GenericInstanceId, CreditedImpls)
typealias ListingStamp = int(0 to u64.max)
typealias ListingStamps = Map with (ByteArray, ListingStamp)

union WitnessCredit
	owner(typeName immutable ByteArray)
	constrainedInstance(instance GenericInstanceId)
	mentionedEnum(enumName immutable ByteArray)
	namedInterface(interfaceName immutable ByteArray)

	function growsWithTheWalk() returns bool
		return match self 'credit'
			owner or
			constrainedInstance gives false
			mentionedEnum or
			namedInterface gives true
		end 'credit'
	end 'growsWithTheWalk'
end 'WitnessCredit'

union WitnessBlame
	functionName
	op(opIndex ElementIndex)
end 'WitnessBlame'

type WitnessCreditEvent
	export let credit as WitnessCredit
	export let blame as WitnessBlame

	static function create(credit WitnessCredit, blame WitnessBlame) returns Self
		return Self{credit: credit, blame: blame}
	end 'create'
end 'WitnessCreditEvent'

module union SubstrateEntryMemo
	unasked
	reachesNone
	reaches(entry immutable ByteArray)

	function prefers(candidate ByteArray) returns bool
		match self 'memo'
			unasked then panic("SubstrateEntryMemo.prefers: asked of a memo nothing has settled")
			reachesNone then return true
			reaches(incumbent) then return substrateEntryIsPreferred(candidate, incumbent: incumbent)
		end 'memo'
	end 'prefers'
end 'SubstrateEntryMemo'

typealias WitnessCreditArray = Array with WitnessCredit
typealias WitnessEventArray = Array with WitnessCreditEvent
typealias EventsByFunction = Map with (ByteArray, WitnessEventArray)
typealias EntryByOp = Map with (ElementIndex, immutable ByteArray)

type CreditedImpls
	export let names as NameRoster
	var substrateEntry as SubstrateEntryMemo

	static function over(names NameRoster) returns Self
		return Self{names: names, substrateEntry: SubstrateEntryMemo.unasked}
	end 'over'

	function settleEntry(entries StdlibSubstrateEntryMap)
		var settled = SubstrateEntryMemo.reachesNone

		for impl in self.names 'eachImpl'
			let entry = try entries.get(impl) otherwise continue

			if settled.prefers(entry) 'aPreferredEntry'
				settled = SubstrateEntryMemo.reaches(entry)
			end 'aPreferredEntry'
		end 'eachImpl'

		self.substrateEntry = settled
	end 'settleEntry'

	function settledEntry() returns immutable ByteArray throws AbsentError
		match self.substrateEntry 'memo'
			unasked then panic("CreditedImpls.settledEntry: asked before the ledger settled its entries")
			reachesNone then throw AbsentError.absent
			reaches(entry) then return entry
		end 'memo'
	end 'settledEntry'
end 'CreditedImpls'

type FunctionScopedSet
	let stamps as ListingStamps
	var scope as ListingStamp

	static function create() returns Self
		return Self{stamps: ListingStamps.create(), scope: 0}
	end 'create'

	function beginScope()
		self.scope = self.scope + 1
	end 'beginScope'

	function claim(name ByteArray) returns bool
		if let stamp = try self.stamps.get(name) 'listed'
			if stamp == self.scope 'alreadyClaimed'
				return false
			end 'alreadyClaimed'
		end 'listed'

		self.stamps.upsert(name, value: self.scope)

		return true
	end 'claim'
end 'FunctionScopedSet'

type LibraryCallResults
	export let values as ValueIdArray
	export let ops as OpIndexArray
	let held as ValueMarkSet

	static function none() returns Self
		return Self{values: ValueIdArray.create(0), ops: OpIndexArray.create(0), held: ValueMarkSet.create()}
	end 'none'

	static function inFunction(func IrFunction, module MaxonModule, stdlib FunctionNameSet) returns Self
		let values = ValueIdArray.create(0)
		let ops = OpIndexArray.create(0)
		let held = ValueMarkSet.create()

		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("LibraryCallResults.inFunction: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")

			for opIdx in block.bodyOps() 'eachOp'
				let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("LibraryCallResults.inFunction: opRef {opIdx} not in module.ops")
				let edge = try callEdgeOf(op) otherwise continue

				if not stdlib.contains(edge.0) 'aProgramCallee'
					continue
				end 'aProgramCallee'

				let result = try maxonOpDefinedValue(op) otherwise panic("LibraryCallResults.inFunction: a call op defines its result, and op {opIdx} names a callee")

				values.push(result)
				ops.push(opIdx)
				held.insert(result)
			end 'eachOp'
		end 'eachBlock'

		return Self{values: values, ops: ops, held: held}
	end 'inFunction'

	function holds(value ValueId) returns bool
		return self.held.contains(value)
	end 'holds'
end 'LibraryCallResults'

module type WitnessCrossings
	module var atFunction as SubstrateEntryMemo
	module let opSites as OpIndexArray
	let opEntries as EntryByOp

	static function none() returns Self
		return Self{atFunction: SubstrateEntryMemo.reachesNone, opSites: OpIndexArray.create(0), opEntries: EntryByOp.create()}
	end 'none'

	function noteAtFunction(entry immutable ByteArray)
		if self.atFunction.prefers(entry) 'aPreferredEntry'
			self.atFunction = SubstrateEntryMemo.reaches(entry)
		end 'aPreferredEntry'
	end 'noteAtFunction'

	function noteAtOp(opIndex ElementIndex, entry immutable ByteArray)
		if let known = try self.opEntries.get(opIndex) 'alreadyBlamed'
			if substrateEntryIsPreferred(entry, incumbent: known) 'aPreferredEntry'
				self.opEntries.upsert(opIndex, value: entry)
			end 'aPreferredEntry'

			return
		end 'alreadyBlamed'

		self.opSites.push(opIndex)
		self.opEntries.upsert(opIndex, value: entry)
	end 'noteAtOp'

	module function entryAtOp(opIndex ElementIndex) returns immutable ByteArray throws MapError
		return try self.opEntries.get(opIndex)
	end 'entryAtOp'
end 'WitnessCrossings'

module type WitnessEvidenceLedger
	var pendingImpls as NameRoster
	let positions as NameRoster
	let functionEvents as WitnessEventArray
	let seedConformers as NameRoster
	let declaredConformers as DeclaredConformanceMap
	let constrainedBases as ConstrainedOwnerSet
	let conformerNameById as ConformerNameById
	let interfacesByEnum as InterfacesByEnum
	let enumsByInterface as EnumConformersByInterface
	let ownerImpls as ImplsByName
	let instanceImpls as ImplsByInstance
	let enumImpls as ImplsByName
	let interfaceImpls as ImplsByName
	let eventsByFunction as EventsByFunction
	let mentions as TypeMentions
	let listedEnums as FunctionScopedSet
	let listedPositions as FunctionScopedSet
	let noCallResults as LibraryCallResults

	static function over(signatures ProgramSignatures, project Project) returns Self
		let constrainedBases = ConstrainedOwnerSet.create()

		for instance in 0 upto signatures.genericInstanceCount() 'eachInstance'
			let baseName = signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(instance)

			if not signatures.structWhereConstraints(baseName).isEmpty() 'constrained'
				constrainedBases.insert(baseName)
			end 'constrained'
		end 'eachInstance'

		let seedConformers = conformersMintedWithoutTheirOwnMethods(signatures)
		let declaredConformers = declaredConformanceIndex(project)
		let conformerNameById = ConformerNameById.create()

		for (conformerName, _) in declaredConformers 'eachDeclaredConformer'
			if seedConformers.contains(conformerName) 'theSeedOwnsIt'
				continue
			end 'theSeedOwnsIt'

			if let id = try project.typeNames.idOf(conformerName) 'interned'
				conformerNameById.upsert(id, value: conformerName)
			end 'interned'
		end 'eachDeclaredConformer'

		let enumsByInterface = enumConformersByInterface(project)
		let interfacesByEnum = InterfacesByEnum.create()

		for (interfaceName, conformers) in enumsByInterface 'eachInterface'
			for enumName in conformers 'eachEnum'
				if let interfaces = try interfacesByEnum.get(enumName) 'alreadyListed'
					interfaces.push(interfaceName)
					interfacesByEnum.upsert(enumName, value: interfaces)
					continue
				end 'alreadyListed'

				let interfaces = NameRoster.create(0)
				interfaces.push(interfaceName)
				interfacesByEnum.upsert(enumName, value: interfaces)
			end 'eachEnum'
		end 'eachInterface'

		return Self.holding(seedConformers, declaredConformers: declaredConformers, constrainedBases: constrainedBases, conformerNameById: conformerNameById, interfacesByEnum: interfacesByEnum, enumsByInterface: enumsByInterface, mentions: TypeMentions.over(signatures))
	end 'over'

	static function none() returns Self
		return Self.holding(NameRoster.create(0), declaredConformers: DeclaredConformanceMap.create(), constrainedBases: ConstrainedOwnerSet.create(), conformerNameById: ConformerNameById.create(), interfacesByEnum: InterfacesByEnum.create(), enumsByInterface: EnumConformersByInterface.create(), mentions: TypeMentions.none())
	end 'none'

	static function holding(seedConformers NameRoster, declaredConformers DeclaredConformanceMap, constrainedBases ConstrainedOwnerSet, conformerNameById ConformerNameById, interfacesByEnum InterfacesByEnum, enumsByInterface EnumConformersByInterface, mentions TypeMentions) returns Self
		return Self{pendingImpls: NameRoster.create(0), positions: NameRoster.create(0), functionEvents: WitnessEventArray.create(0), seedConformers: seedConformers, declaredConformers: declaredConformers, constrainedBases: constrainedBases, conformerNameById: conformerNameById, interfacesByEnum: interfacesByEnum, enumsByInterface: enumsByInterface, ownerImpls: ImplsByName.create(), instanceImpls: ImplsByInstance.create(), enumImpls: ImplsByName.create(), interfaceImpls: ImplsByName.create(), eventsByFunction: EventsByFunction.create(), mentions: mentions, listedEnums: FunctionScopedSet.create(), listedPositions: FunctionScopedSet.create(), noCallResults: LibraryCallResults.none()}
	end 'holding'

	function noteFunction(func IrFunction, module MaxonModule, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, stdlib FunctionNameSet)
		self.functionEvents.clear()

		if let typeName = try witnessOwnerOf(func.name) 'anOwnedFunction'
			self.noteCredit(WitnessCredit.owner(typeName), blame: WitnessBlame.functionName, project: project, signatures: signatures)
		end 'anOwnedFunction'

		self.positions.clear()
		self.listedPositions.beginScope()
		collectWitnessPositionInterfaces(func, project: project, signatures: signatures, funcSignatures: project.funcSignatures, into: self.positions, listed: self.listedPositions)

		for interfaceName in self.positions 'eachNamedInterface'
			self.noteCredit(WitnessCredit.namedInterface(interfaceName), blame: WitnessBlame.functionName, project: project, signatures: signatures)
		end 'eachNamedInterface'

		self.noteMentionsOf(func, module: module, project: project, signatures: signatures, stdlib: stdlib)

		if self.functionEvents.count() == 0 'creditedNothing'
			return
		end 'creditedNothing'

		let events = WitnessEventArray.create(0)
		events.append(self.functionEvents)
		self.eventsByFunction.upsert(func.name, value: events)
	end 'noteFunction'

	function takeLiveImpls() returns NameRoster
		let taken = self.pendingImpls
		self.pendingImpls = NameRoster.create(0)

		return taken
	end 'takeLiveImpls'

	function settle(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures)
		for enumName in namesIn(self.enumImpls) 'eachMentionedEnum'
			self.enumImpls.upsert(enumName, value: CreditedImpls.over(self.enumImplsOf(enumName, project: project, signatures: signatures)))
		end 'eachMentionedEnum'

		for interfaceName in namesIn(self.interfaceImpls) 'eachNamedInterface'
			self.interfaceImpls.upsert(interfaceName, value: CreditedImpls.over(self.interfaceImplsOf(interfaceName, project: project, signatures: signatures)))
		end 'eachNamedInterface'
	end 'settle'

	function settleEntries(entries StdlibSubstrateEntryMap)
		settleEntriesIn(self.ownerImpls, entries: entries)
		settleEntriesIn(self.enumImpls, entries: entries)
		settleEntriesIn(self.interfaceImpls, entries: entries)

		for (_, credited) in self.instanceImpls 'eachConstrainedInstance'
			credited.settleEntry(entries)
		end 'eachConstrainedInstance'
	end 'settleEntries'

	function creditsOf(funcName immutable ByteArray) returns WitnessEventArray
		return try self.eventsByFunction.get(funcName) otherwise WitnessEventArray.create(0)
	end 'creditsOf'

	function implsOf(credit WitnessCredit) returns NameRoster
		let credited = try self.creditedImplsOf(credit) otherwise return NameRoster.create(0)
		return credited.names
	end 'implsOf'

	module function liveImplsOf(funcName immutable ByteArray) returns NameRoster
		let impls = NameRoster.create(0)

		for event in self.creditsOf(funcName) 'eachCredit'
			impls.append(self.implsOf(event.credit))
		end 'eachCredit'

		return impls
	end 'liveImplsOf'

	function substrateEntryOf(credit WitnessCredit) returns immutable ByteArray throws AbsentError
		let credited = try self.creditedImplsOf(credit)
		return try credited.settledEntry()
	end 'substrateEntryOf'

	function creditedImplsOf(credit WitnessCredit) returns CreditedImpls throws AbsentError
		match credit 'route'
			owner(typeName) then return try self.ownerImpls.get(typeName) otherwise throw AbsentError.absent
			constrainedInstance(instance) then return try self.instanceImpls.get(instance) otherwise throw AbsentError.absent
			mentionedEnum(enumName) then return try self.enumImpls.get(enumName) otherwise throw AbsentError.absent
			namedInterface(interfaceName) then return try self.interfaceImpls.get(interfaceName) otherwise throw AbsentError.absent
		end 'route'
	end 'creditedImplsOf'

	function enumImplsOf(enumName ByteArray, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns NameRoster
		let impls = NameRoster.create(0)
		let interfaces = try self.interfacesByEnum.get(enumName) otherwise panic("WitnessEvidenceLedger.enumImplsOf: '{String.from(enumName)}' was filed because some interface lists it as a conformer")

		for interfaceName in interfaces 'eachDeclaredInterface'
			if self.interfaceImpls.contains(interfaceName) 'named'
				impls.append(witnessImplNamesOf(enumName, interfaceName: interfaceName, project: project, signatures: signatures))
			end 'named'
		end 'eachDeclaredInterface'

		return impls
	end 'enumImplsOf'

	function interfaceImplsOf(interfaceName ByteArray, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns NameRoster
		let impls = NameRoster.create(0)

		for conformerName in self.seedConformers 'eachSeededConformer'
			impls.append(witnessImplNamesOf(conformerName, interfaceName: interfaceName, project: project, signatures: signatures))
		end 'eachSeededConformer'

		if let enumNames = try self.enumsByInterface.get(interfaceName) 'someEnumDeclaresIt'
			for enumName in enumNames 'eachEnumConformer'
				if self.enumImpls.contains(enumName) 'aValueMayExist'
					impls.append(witnessImplNamesOf(enumName, interfaceName: interfaceName, project: project, signatures: signatures))
				end 'aValueMayExist'
			end 'eachEnumConformer'
		end 'someEnumDeclaresIt'

		return impls
	end 'interfaceImplsOf'

	function mentionCredits(signatures ProgramSignatures) returns WitnessCreditArray
		let credits = WitnessCreditArray.create(0)

		self.listedEnums.beginScope()

		for instance in self.mentions.instances 'eachInstance'
			if self.constrainedBases.contains(signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(instance)) 'aConstrainedInstance'
				credits.push(WitnessCredit.constrainedInstance(instance))
			end 'aConstrainedInstance'
		end 'eachInstance'

		for typeId in self.mentions.typeIds 'eachNamedType'
			if let conformerName = try self.conformerNameById.get(typeId) 'aDeclaredConformer'
				self.listEnumCredit(conformerName, into: credits)
			end 'aDeclaredConformer'
		end 'eachNamedType'

		for typeName in self.mentions.argumentTypeNames 'eachNamedArgument'
			if self.declaredConformers.contains(typeName) and not self.seedConformers.contains(typeName) 'aDeclaredConformer'
				self.listEnumCredit(typeName, into: credits)
			end 'aDeclaredConformer'
		end 'eachNamedArgument'

		return credits
	end 'mentionCredits'

	function listEnumCredit(conformerName immutable ByteArray, into WitnessCreditArray)
		if self.interfacesByEnum.contains(conformerName) and self.listedEnums.claim(conformerName) 'anEnumNotYetListed'
			into.push(WitnessCredit.mentionedEnum(conformerName))
		end 'anEnumNotYetListed'
	end 'listEnumCredit'

	function noteMentionsOf(func IrFunction, module MaxonModule, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, stdlib FunctionNameSet)
		if self.constrainedBases.count() == 0 and self.declaredConformers.count() == 0 'nothingToCredit'
			return
		end 'nothingToCredit'

		let callResults = self.callResultsOf(func, module: module, stdlib: stdlib)

		for position in 0 upto callResults.values.count() 'eachCallResult'
			self.mentions.gatherValue(func, value: try callResults.values.get(position) otherwise panic("WitnessEvidenceLedger.noteMentionsOf: values.get OOB at {position}"), project: project, signatures: signatures)
			self.noteMentionCredits(WitnessBlame.op(try callResults.ops.get(position) otherwise panic("WitnessEvidenceLedger.noteMentionsOf: ops.get OOB at {position}")), project: project, signatures: signatures)
		end 'eachCallResult'

		self.mentions.gather(func, project: project, signatures: signatures, skipping: callResults)
		self.noteMentionCredits(WitnessBlame.functionName, project: project, signatures: signatures)
	end 'noteMentionsOf'

	function callResultsOf(func IrFunction, module MaxonModule, stdlib FunctionNameSet) returns LibraryCallResults
		if stdlib.contains(func.name) 'noOpIsBlamed'
			return self.noCallResults
		end 'noOpIsBlamed'

		return LibraryCallResults.inFunction(func, module: module, stdlib: stdlib)
	end 'callResultsOf'

	function noteMentionCredits(blame WitnessBlame, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures)
		for credit in self.mentionCredits(signatures) 'eachMentionCredit'
			self.noteCredit(credit, blame: blame, project: project, signatures: signatures)
		end 'eachMentionCredit'
	end 'noteMentionCredits'

	function noteCredit(credit WitnessCredit, blame WitnessBlame, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures)
		let impls = self.admit(credit, project: project, signatures: signatures)

		if impls.count() > 0 or credit.growsWithTheWalk() 'aCreditThisFunctionOwes'
			self.functionEvents.push(WitnessCreditEvent.create(credit, blame: blame))
		end 'aCreditThisFunctionOwes'
	end 'noteCredit'

	function admit(credit WitnessCredit, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns NameRoster
		if let known = try self.creditedImplsOf(credit) 'alreadyAdmitted'
			return known.names
		end 'alreadyAdmitted'

		let impls = self.impliedImpls(credit, project: project, signatures: signatures)
		self.enrol(credit, impls: impls)
		self.pendingImpls.append(impls)

		return impls
	end 'admit'

	function impliedImpls(credit WitnessCredit, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns NameRoster
		match credit 'route'
			owner(typeName) then return ownerWitnessImplNames(typeName, seedConformers: self.seedConformers, project: project, signatures: signatures)
			constrainedInstance(instance) then return constraintWitnessImplNames(instance, project: project, signatures: signatures)
			mentionedEnum(enumName) then return self.enumImplsOf(enumName, project: project, signatures: signatures)
			namedInterface(interfaceName) then return self.interfaceImplsOf(interfaceName, project: project, signatures: signatures)
		end 'route'
	end 'impliedImpls'

	function enrol(credit WitnessCredit, impls NameRoster)
		match credit 'route'
			owner(typeName) then self.ownerImpls.upsert(typeName, value: CreditedImpls.over(impls))
			constrainedInstance(instance) then self.instanceImpls.upsert(instance, value: CreditedImpls.over(impls))
			mentionedEnum(enumName) then self.enumImpls.upsert(enumName, value: CreditedImpls.over(impls))
			namedInterface(interfaceName) then self.interfaceImpls.upsert(interfaceName, value: CreditedImpls.over(impls))
		end 'route'
	end 'enrol'
end 'WitnessEvidenceLedger'

function namesIn(table ImplsByName) returns NameRoster
	let names = NameRoster.create(0)

	for (name, _) in table 'eachName'
		names.push(name)
	end 'eachName'

	return names
end 'namesIn'

function settleEntriesIn(table ImplsByName, entries StdlibSubstrateEntryMap)
	for (_, credited) in table 'eachCredited'
		credited.settleEntry(entries)
	end 'eachCredited'
end 'settleEntriesIn'

// ⭐ THE FACTS ABOUT LIBRARY SOURCE A COMPILE DERIVES, from ONE walk of the merged module. They are one
// object rather than separate `Project` fields because they are one derivation over one call graph:
// `substrateEntries` is meaningful only for the functions `unreachable` does NOT hold (code the program
// cannot reach is invisible to it, substrate and all), `unreachable` and `unreachedRuntimeTier` are the
// same reach answer split by tier, and fields assigned from separate calls could come to disagree about
// which functions those are.
export type LibraryFacts
	// Every function the merged module got from LIBRARY source — `stdlib/` and its sibling `runtime/`
	// alike, which are one population to every reader of this set. Materialized once so the question can
	// be asked of every function of the program without paying a path comparison each time (see
	// `isLibraryFunction`).
	export let libraryFunctions as FunctionNameSet

	// Stdlib functions NOT reachable from `main` — the functions whose BODY this compile does not build.
	// Dead-function elimination drops them before the back end, so a pass that runs earlier and examines
	// function BODIES to decide something about the PROGRAM — the runtime floor it carries
	// (`scanRuntimeUsage`), the targets it can be built for (`checkCalls`) — must not let them decide it,
	// or the program pays for code it does not contain. User functions are never in it: a diagnostic in
	// unreached USER code is still the user's bug, while a runtime requirement of unreached STDLIB code is
	// nobody's. ⛔ **AND NO `runtime/` FUNCTION IS EVER IN IT** — `classifyLibraryReach` carries that
	// exemption and why it is by PROVENANCE rather than by reachability; an unreached tier name is filed
	// under `unreachedRuntimeTier` instead, which answers the narrower question its readers may ask.
	export let unreachable as FunctionNameSet

	// The REACHABLE stdlib functions that reach a host entry this compile's target lacks, and which
	// entry each one reaches. Read at a call site that crosses from user code into stdlib, to refuse the program on
	// a target that cannot lower it — at the user's own span.
	export let substrateEntries as StdlibSubstrateEntryMap

	// The `runtime/` half of `libraryFunctions` — the tier whose bodies the compiler reaches BY NAME and no
	// source file may call. It is provenance, so every question of the form *"is this callee the runtime's
	// rather than the program's?"* reads it: `classifyLibraryReach`, which may never file one under
	// `unreachable`, and the yield walk's fallback, which must answer about a runtime entry exactly as it
	// did when the entry had no body in any source. One derivation, because a second walk over the same
	// files could come to disagree about which names are in the tier.
	export let runtimeTier as FunctionNameSet

	// The `runtimeTier` names no root of this program reaches. BUILT and REACHED are different questions
	// for the tier and only this field answers the second: a tier body is built unconditionally, so it is
	// never `unreachable`, yet dead-function elimination still sweeps one nothing calls. A pass that reads
	// a BODY to decide what the PROGRAM carries (`scanRuntimeUsage`) must skip it, or the program pays a
	// `.data` word or a host chunk for code it does not contain.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **OVER-APPROXIMATION IS THE SAFE DIRECTION AND THE OTHER ONE IS A LINK FAILURE.** A name missing
	// here costs bytes; a name present here while the body survives leaves that body reading a slot the
	// image never laid out. So a name enters ONLY on the walk's positive evidence that nothing reaches it,
	// and every edge the walk does not model resolves to "reached".
	// `DeadFunctionElimination.requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead` is where the unsafe direction is caught.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE COMPILER-CALLED TIER FILES JOIN IT LATE** (`withCompilerCalledTierUnreached`): their reach is
	// declared by the installer that mints the calls, and that installer's gate is not known until the usage
	// record is final — after `scanRuntimeUsage`, before lowering.
	export let unreachedRuntimeTier as FunctionNameSet

	// The `runtimeTier` names `runtimeTierFileIsCompilerCalled` DECLARES reached, because no walk over
	// source can see a call an INSTALLER mints. It is carried rather than consumed and dropped, because the
	// declaration stands on a minter that may not run: whether the allocator is installed is settled long
	// after this derivation, and `withCompilerCalledTierUnreached` is where the two are joined.
	export let compilerCalledTier as FunctionNameSet

	let substrateNodes as NameRoster

	module let witnessEvidence as WitnessEvidenceLedger

	static function create(libraryFunctions FunctionNameSet, unreachable FunctionNameSet, substrateEntries StdlibSubstrateEntryMap, runtimeTier FunctionNameSet, unreachedRuntimeTier FunctionNameSet, compilerCalledTier FunctionNameSet, substrateNodes NameRoster, witnessEvidence WitnessEvidenceLedger) returns LibraryFacts
		return Self{libraryFunctions: libraryFunctions, unreachable: unreachable, substrateEntries: substrateEntries, runtimeTier: runtimeTier, unreachedRuntimeTier: unreachedRuntimeTier, compilerCalledTier: compilerCalledTier, substrateNodes: substrateNodes, witnessEvidence: witnessEvidence}
	end 'create'

	// A compile that has not derived them yet, and the answer for a compile that loaded no stdlib source
	// at all: no function is stdlib, nothing is unreachable-stdlib, nothing is in the runtime tier and
	// nothing needs the substrate.
	export static function empty() returns LibraryFacts
		return Self.create(FunctionNameSet.create(), unreachable: FunctionNameSet.create(), substrateEntries: StdlibSubstrateEntryMap.create(), runtimeTier: FunctionNameSet.create(), unreachedRuntimeTier: FunctionNameSet.create(), compilerCalledTier: FunctionNameSet.create(), substrateNodes: NameRoster.create(0), witnessEvidence: WitnessEvidenceLedger.none())
	end 'empty'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE QUESTION EVERY PRE-ELIMINATION PASS ASKS OF A LIBRARY BODY: WILL DEAD-FUNCTION
	// ELIMINATION SWEEP IT?** `unreachable` and `unreachedRuntimeTier` are one reach answer split by tier,
	// and a pass that lowers, guards or scans a body — and so registers `.rdata` or credits a runtime floor
	// for it — must skip exactly their union, or the program pays for code it does not contain. Elimination
	// prunes FUNCTIONS, never `.rdata`, so a blob minted for a body it sweeps SURVIVES the sweep, and one
	// shared counter means a surviving blob renumbers every later `__str_blob_`/`__jumptable_` label in the
	// program. `DeadFunctionElimination.requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead` refuses a compile where the
	// sweep keeps anything this answers for.
	export function bodyIsSwept(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.unreachable.contains(name) or self.unreachedRuntimeTier.contains(name)
	end 'bodyIsSwept'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE DECLARED REACH EDGE, SETTLED — A STRICTLY SHARPER ANSWER THAN THE WALK CAN GIVE, AND ONE
	// IT CANNOT BE ASKED FOR WHERE IT RUNS.** `compilerCalledTier` is credited reached at derivation time
	// because the only alternative there is to guess; the declaration stands on a MINTER, and the slab
	// family's (`SlabRuntime.installSlabRuntime`) emits nothing unless the program allocates. So in a program
	// with no allocator every name of those files is filed unreached — their bodies lower no `.rdata` and
	// insert no guard, exactly as any other swept library body.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT IS ASKED ONLY ONCE `usesHeap` IS FINAL** (`prepareLowering`, past every arm that
	// can still turn it on) **AND AFTER `scanRuntimeUsage` HAS RUN**, because the direction of risk inverts
	// between the two readers: over-approximating what the SCAN treats as unreached leaves a body calling
	// into a floor nothing installed, while over-approximating what is SWEPT skips the lowering of a body the
	// program calls. `requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead` is what turns the second into a refusal rather
	// than an empty callee.
	export function withCompilerCalledTierUnreached(sweepAllocatorTier bool) returns LibraryFacts
		let unreached = FunctionNameSet.create()

		for name in self.unreachedRuntimeTier 'eachAlreadyUnreached'
			unreached.insert(name)
		end 'eachAlreadyUnreached'

		if sweepAllocatorTier 'noInstallerMintsAnAllocatorCall'
			for name in self.compilerCalledTier 'eachCompilerCalled'
				unreached.insert(name)
			end 'eachCompilerCalled'
		end 'noInstallerMintsAnAllocatorCall'

		return Self.create(self.libraryFunctions, unreachable: self.unreachable, substrateEntries: self.substrateEntries, runtimeTier: self.runtimeTier, unreachedRuntimeTier: unreached, compilerCalledTier: self.compilerCalledTier, substrateNodes: self.substrateNodes, witnessEvidence: self.witnessEvidence)
	end 'withCompilerCalledTierUnreached'

	export function withSubstrateEntriesOf(module MaxonModule, target Target) returns LibraryFacts
		let entries = stdlibSubstrateEntries(module, stdlibNames: self.substrateNodes, unreachable: self.unreachable, evidence: self.witnessEvidence, target: target)
		self.witnessEvidence.settleEntries(entries)

		return Self.create(self.libraryFunctions, unreachable: self.unreachable, substrateEntries: entries, runtimeTier: self.runtimeTier, unreachedRuntimeTier: self.unreachedRuntimeTier, compilerCalledTier: self.compilerCalledTier, substrateNodes: self.substrateNodes, witnessEvidence: self.witnessEvidence)
	end 'withSubstrateEntriesOf'

	module function witnessCrossingsOf(project Project, module MaxonModule, func IrFunction) returns WitnessCrossings
		let crossings = WitnessCrossings.none()

		for event in self.witnessEvidence.creditsOf(func.name) 'eachCredit'
			let entry = try self.witnessEvidence.substrateEntryOf(event.credit) otherwise continue

			match event.blame 'site'
				functionName then crossings.noteAtFunction(entry)
				op(opIndex) then self.noteCrossingAtOp(crossings, opIndex: opIndex, entry: entry, project: project, module: module, func: func)
			end 'site'
		end 'eachCredit'

		return crossings
	end 'witnessCrossingsOf'

	function noteCrossingAtOp(crossings WitnessCrossings, opIndex ElementIndex, entry immutable ByteArray, project Project, module MaxonModule, func IrFunction)
		if opIsTheCompilersOwn(project, func: func, opIndex: opIndex) 'aCallTheCompilerMinted'
			crossings.noteAtFunction(entry)
			return
		end 'aCallTheCompilerMinted'

		let op = try module.ops.get(opIndex) otherwise panic("LibraryFacts.noteCrossingAtOp: ops.get OOB at {opIndex}")
		let edge = try callEdgeOf(op) otherwise panic("LibraryFacts.noteCrossingAtOp: op {opIndex} was blamed as a call and names no callee")

		if self.substrateEntries.contains(edge.0) 'theCalleeIsRefusedItself'
			return
		end 'theCalleeIsRefusedItself'

		crossings.noteAtOp(opIndex, entry: entry)
	end 'noteCrossingAtOp'
end 'LibraryFacts'

// ⭐ DERIVE THE REACH FACTS, all but `substrateEntries` (see `withSubstrateEntriesOf`). Runs once
// per compile, right after the module is merged and before any pass that reads them — and before
// `DeadGlobalElimination.liveGlobalLabels`, which is the earliest reader
// (`prepareLowering`).
//
// It takes `signatures` for one thing: the functions a top-level initializer calls. Those calls are
// made by `__module_init`, which does not exist yet at this point and is called by the entry stub
// rather than from `main` once it does, so neither `libraryReachOf`'s walk nor its short-circuit
// can see the edge in any body. It is read off the declaration that causes the call instead
// (`ProgramSignatures.globalInitCallees`), which is also what lets this run before the synthesis:
// see that function for why the answer does not depend on the global liveness this derivation
// decides.
export function deriveLibraryFacts(project Project, module MaxonModule, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns LibraryFacts throws CompileError
	let reach = try libraryReachOf(project, module: module, signatures: signatures)

	return match reach 'reach'
		noLibrarySource gives LibraryFacts.empty()
		unreferenced(roster, stdlib) gives libraryFactsOf(roster, stdlib: stdlib, split: classifyLibraryReach(roster, reachable: FunctionNameSet.create()), substrateNodes: NameRoster.create(0), evidence: WitnessEvidenceLedger.none())
		walked(roster, stdlib, reachable, ledger) gives libraryFactsOf(roster, stdlib: stdlib, split: classifyLibraryReach(roster, reachable: reachable), substrateNodes: roster.names, evidence: ledger)
	end 'reach'
end 'deriveLibraryFacts'

function libraryFactsOf(roster LibraryFunctionRoster, stdlib FunctionNameSet, split LibraryReachSplit, substrateNodes NameRoster, evidence WitnessEvidenceLedger) returns LibraryFacts
	return LibraryFacts.create(stdlib, unreachable: split.unreachable, substrateEntries: StdlibSubstrateEntryMap.create(), runtimeTier: roster.runtimeTier, unreachedRuntimeTier: split.unreachedRuntimeTier, compilerCalledTier: roster.compilerReached, substrateNodes: substrateNodes, witnessEvidence: evidence)
end 'libraryFactsOf'

module union LibraryReach
	noLibrarySource
	unreferenced(roster LibraryFunctionRoster, stdlib FunctionNameSet)
	walked(roster LibraryFunctionRoster, stdlib FunctionNameSet, reachable FunctionNameSet, ledger WitnessEvidenceLedger)
end 'LibraryReach'

module function reachedLibraryNamesOf(reach LibraryReach) returns FunctionNameSet
	return match reach 'reach'
		noLibrarySource or
		unreferenced gives FunctionNameSet.create()
		walked(_, _, reachable, _) gives reachable
	end 'reach'
end 'reachedLibraryNamesOf'

module function libraryReachOf(project Project, module MaxonModule, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns LibraryReach throws CompileError
	// Only an unreadable type universe skips the walk, because the library it would walk is half read.
	// Any other program with errors is still walked: without its reach no library body is built, every
	// library callee reads as worst-case, and the borrow checks bury the program's errors under
	// thousands that are not its own.
	if project.poison.typeUniverseIsUnreadable() 'theLibraryIsHalfRead'
		return LibraryReach.noLibrarySource
	end 'theLibraryIsHalfRead'

	let roster = try libraryFunctionRoster(project)

	if roster.names.count() == 0 'noStdlibSource'
		return LibraryReach.noLibrarySource
	end 'noStdlibSource'

	let stdlibSet = FunctionNameSet.create()

	for name in roster.names 'seed'
		stdlibSet.insert(name)
	end 'seed'

	// Built once, here, and handed to both walks below: the short-circuit and the precise walk ask
	// different questions of the same list, and a second build would be the same arena walked twice.
	let initCallees = signatures.globalInitCallees()

	// Under `check-scriptlib` every package function, and under `check-stdlib` every stdlib function, is a
	// reach root: nothing calls one there, and a diagnostic in a swept library body is dropped.
	for name in roster.auditedLibrary 'eachAuditedLibraryFunction'
		initCallees.push(name)
	end 'eachAuditedLibraryFunction'

	// Common-case short-circuit. If no initializer calls and no op in user code reaches stdlib, no
	// stdlib function is reachable at all, so an empty reached set is the exact answer and none can be
	// blamed for a substrate. It is one allocation-free O(ops) scan, without the O(functions)
	// name->index map and reached-array the from-`main` walk allocates, and without the substrate
	// fixpoint. That map is the whole per-compile memory cost of loading stdlib source into a program
	// that never touches it, so skipping it keeps the cost a per-compile constant instead of growing
	// with the user program. It stays exact: a stdlib function referenced by nothing is reachable from
	// nothing.
	if initCallees.count() == 0 and not userCodeReachesStdlib(module, project: project, signatures: signatures, stdlib: stdlibSet, initCallees: initCallees) 'noneReferenced'
		return LibraryReach.unreferenced(roster, stdlib: stdlibSet)
	end 'noneReferenced'

	let ledger = WitnessEvidenceLedger.over(signatures, project: project)
	let reachable = try reachableMaxonFunctionNames(module, project: project, signatures: signatures, stdlib: stdlibSet, initCallees: initCallees, ledger: ledger)
	ledger.settle(project, signatures: signatures)

	return LibraryReach.walked(roster, stdlib: stdlibSet, reachable: reachable, ledger: ledger)
end 'libraryReachOf'

// The fixpoint step `FrontEndRun` iterates, answering whether the demand grew.
//
// ⚠ It asks `deriveLibraryFacts`' question of the same walk (`libraryReachOf`): the set of bodies
// this compile builds and the set `lowerMaxonToStd` lowers must be decided by one derivation, or a
// body could be lowered that was never parsed. Round 0 merges every file with no stdlib body built,
// so the walk sees the user's own edges into stdlib and stops there; each later round has the
// bodies the previous one demanded, so the walk goes one call deeper. It terminates because the
// demand only ever grows and is bounded by the stdlib functions that exist.
module function growStdlibBodyDemand(project Project, reach LibraryReach) returns bool throws CompileError
	match reach 'reach'
		noLibrarySource or
		unreferenced then return false

		walked(_, _, reachable, _) then return try demandEveryReachedLibraryFile(project, reachable: reachable)
	end 'reach'
end 'growStdlibBodyDemand'

function demandEveryReachedLibraryFile(project Project, reachable FunctionNameSet) returns bool throws CompileError
	var grew = false

	for fileIndex in 0 upto project.librarySourcePaths.count() 'eachFile'
		let demandedThisFile = try demandOneStdlibFileIfReached(project, path: try project.librarySourcePaths.get(fileIndex) otherwise panic("demandEveryReachedLibraryFile: fileIndex {fileIndex} is below project.librarySourcePaths.count()"), reachable: reachable)
		grew = grew or demandedThisFile
	end 'eachFile'

	return grew
end 'demandEveryReachedLibraryFile'

// Demand ONE stdlib file's bodies if any function it declares is reachable, answering whether the demand
// set grew.
function demandOneStdlibFileIfReached(project Project, path FilePath, reachable FunctionNameSet) returns bool throws CompileError
	if project.db.demandedLibraryBodyFiles.contains(path) 'alreadyBuilding'
		return false
	end 'alreadyBuilding'

	// `runtime/`, a scriptlib under `check-scriptlib` and a stdlib under `check-stdlib` are outside the
	// demand entirely — their bodies are built unconditionally (`Queries.buildsBodiesFor`). A record here
	// would be read by nobody and would spin the fixpoint one further round.
	if isRuntimeSourceFile(project, path: path) or libraryBodiesAreEveryBody(project, path: path) 'notTheDemandTier'
		return false
	end 'notTheDemandTier'

	let artifact = try queryParseOps(project, path: path)

	for func in artifact.module.functions 'eachFunc'
		if not reachable.contains(func.name) 'notReached'
			continue
		end 'notReached'

		project.db.demandedLibraryBodyFiles.insert(path)
		return true
	end 'eachFunc'

	return false
end 'demandOneStdlibFileIfReached'

// Every function the merged module got from library source, WITH the tier each one was declared in. The
// two columns are built by ONE walk because the tier is a property of the FILE the first column is read
// out of; asking it afterwards would need a name->path map nothing else here has a use for.
module type LibraryFunctionRoster
	// Every library function, in load order. A `NameRoster` rather than a set because the names are
	// unique by construction (E3006 rejects a duplicate) and the substrate fixpoint needs a dense ORDER
	// for its node space as well as membership.
	export let names as NameRoster

	// The `runtime/` subset of `names` — the tier `classifyLibraryReach` may never file `unreachable`.
	export let runtimeTier as FunctionNameSet

	// The tier names a COMPILER-MINTED call reaches — the declared half of the reach answer, filled by the
	// same walk as the two columns above so the three cannot come to disagree about which names they hold.
	export let compilerReached as FunctionNameSet

	export let auditedLibrary as NameRoster

	static function create(names NameRoster, runtimeTier FunctionNameSet, compilerReached FunctionNameSet, auditedLibrary NameRoster) returns LibraryFunctionRoster
		return Self{names: names, runtimeTier: runtimeTier, compilerReached: compilerReached, auditedLibrary: auditedLibrary}
	end 'create'
end 'LibraryFunctionRoster'

// Read back from each library file's PARSE ARTIFACT — a memo HIT, since `FrontEndRun` has already
// parsed it — so the cost is O(library functions) and touches nothing the user wrote.
function libraryFunctionRoster(project Project) returns LibraryFunctionRoster throws CompileError
	let names = NameRoster.create(0)
	let runtimeTier = FunctionNameSet.create()
	let compilerReached = FunctionNameSet.create()
	let auditedLibrary = NameRoster.create(0)

	for fileIndex in 0 upto project.librarySourcePaths.count() 'eachFile'
		try collectOneLibraryFile(project, path: try project.librarySourcePaths.get(fileIndex) otherwise panic("libraryFunctionRoster: fileIndex {fileIndex} is below project.librarySourcePaths.count()"), names: names, runtimeTier: runtimeTier, compilerReached: compilerReached, auditedLibrary: auditedLibrary)
	end 'eachFile'

	return LibraryFunctionRoster.create(names, runtimeTier: runtimeTier, compilerReached: compilerReached, auditedLibrary: auditedLibrary)
end 'libraryFunctionRoster'

// One library file's functions, filed into the roster's columns.
function collectOneLibraryFile(project Project, path FilePath, names NameRoster, runtimeTier FunctionNameSet, compilerReached FunctionNameSet, auditedLibrary NameRoster) throws CompileError
	let isRuntime = isRuntimeSourceFile(project, path: path)
	let isAuditedLibrary = libraryBodiesAreEveryBody(project, path: path)
	let isCompilerCalled = isRuntime and runtimeTierFileIsCompilerCalled(path)
	let artifact = try queryParseOps(project, path: path)

	for func in artifact.module.functions 'eachFunc'
		names.push(func.name)

		if isRuntime 'theRuntimeTier'
			runtimeTier.insert(func.name)
		end 'theRuntimeTier'

		if isCompilerCalled 'declaredReached'
			compilerReached.insert(func.name)
		end 'declaredReached'

		if isAuditedLibrary 'anAuditedLibraryFunction'
			auditedLibrary.push(func.name)
		end 'anAuditedLibraryFunction'
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'collectOneLibraryFile'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE DECLARED REACH EDGE: WHICH TIER FILES THE COMPILER ITSELF CALLS INTO.**
// `RuntimeUsage`'s rule 2 is what decides that this is a roster and not a walk — *"A CALL THE WALK CANNOT
// SEE IS DECLARED; EVERY CALL IT CAN SEE IS DISCOVERED."* Every other tier family is kept alive by a call
// the PARSER mints from user source (`__Builtins.cpuCount()` ⇒ `__cpu_count`), which is an ordinary edge in
// an ordinary body and the from-`main` walk finds it. These files' callers are `StdOp.call` sites an
// INSTALLER mints into the appended band (`afterPipeline`), long after this derivation has
// run and into a module that is not the Maxon one — so the walk holds no evidence about them at all, and
// every one of their entries would be filed `unreachedRuntimeTier` while the bodies ship. The slab's two
// are minted by `installSlabRuntime`; `ManagedGeometry.maxon`'s three are minted by the managed-memory,
// file and socket builders, which emit a `__mg_*` call; `WideText.maxon`'s are minted by
// `HostTextRuntime`'s builders on a wide host.
//
// ⛔⛔ **A FILE LISTED HERE IS WALKED BY `scanRuntimeUsage` IN EVERY PROGRAM, SO THE BAND ITS ENTRIES WEAR
// IS A USAGE DECISION.** The scan reads a listed body's calls and sets the bits their NAMES ask for —
// `MmRuntime.isRuntimeCallee` turns a `__mm_` callee into `usesHeap`. A geometry helper named `__mm_*`
// would therefore set `usesHeap` in a program that allocates nothing, shipping the allocator's `.data`
// state word. ⇒ **A TIER FAMILY ADDED HERE MUST WEAR A BAND NO `recordCallUsage` ARM CLAIMS**, unless
// it really does need what that arm installs.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT IS BY FILE AND NOT BY NAME, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES IT SOUND ON *BOTH* PATHS.** An installer's
// call lands on an ENTRY POINT whose body then reaches that file's module-scoped helpers. On the precise
// path the walk would close over those for free, but `deriveLibraryFacts`' short-circuit hands
// `classifyLibraryReach` an EMPTY reached set — and a program can allocate while naming no stdlib function
// at all, because `__mm_alloc` is no stdlib declaration. A name roster would credit the eight entries there
// and file their helpers unreached, with the bodies still shipping. A FILE is closed over its own helpers by
// construction.
//
// ⛔⛔ **OVER-APPROXIMATION IS THE SAFE DIRECTION AND THE WALK TAKES IT OUTRIGHT: THE ROSTER IS
// UNCONDITIONAL HERE.** A name credited reached in a program that cannot reach it costs BYTES —
// `scanRuntimeUsage` reads the bodies and sets the bits their calls ask for. A name filed unreached while
// its body ships is a call into a floor nothing installed, which is a link failure. The heap cannot be asked
// at this point: `usesHeap` has producers (`--async-trace`, `--debugstream`) that run AFTER the scan, and one
// of those missed is exactly the unsafe direction. ⇒ The gate is asked once it is final instead —
// `LibraryFacts.withCompilerCalledTierUnreached`, from `prepareLowering` before lowering — so a
// program the installer will mint no call for lowers none of these bodies and registers none of their
// `.rdata` (`specs/stdlib-loading.md`'s `a-stdlib-modules-literals-cannot-reach-the-rdata-image` is the
// gate: the arena's panic strings must not lead a heap-free program's read-only image).
//
// ⚠ **A DECLARED ROSTER CAN GO STALE, AND WHAT KEEPS THIS ONE HONEST IS ONE GUARD AND ONE DIRECTION.**
// `DeadFunctionElimination.requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead` panics BY NAME the moment elimination
// reaches a body this roster left out — so a tier file the compiler starts calling into and nobody lists
// here fails loudly on the first program that allocates.
//
// ⭐ **THE OTHER DIRECTION IS NOT "NO WRONG ANSWER" — IT IS `.rdata` IN FRONT OF THE PROGRAM'S OWN.** A
// file listed here whose minter does not run for THIS program has every one of its bodies swept, and the
// blobs the pre-elimination passes registered for them survive the sweep — which is why the roster is
// settled against the minter's own gate rather than left as the walk credited it.
function runtimeTierFileIsCompilerCalled(path FilePath) returns bool
	let filename = path.filename()

	return filename == SlabArenaTierFileName or filename == SlabRuntimeTierFileName or filename == ManagedGeometryTierFileName or filename == WideTextTierFileName
end 'runtimeTierFileIsCompilerCalled'

// ⚠ `fileName` must be unique across `stdlib/` and `runtime/`: the first file the roster lists under it
// answers.
//
// The roster is walked rather than the path rebuilt from `project.runtimeDir`, because a constant is filed
// under the identical `FilePath` the loader handed the parser. A path that differs only by a separator or a
// normalisation misses every declaration in the file, and the lookup then accuses it of declaring nothing.
export function librarySourcePath(project Project, fileName String) returns FilePath
	for fileIndex in 0 upto project.librarySourcePaths.count() 'eachFile'
		let path = try project.librarySourcePaths.get(fileIndex) otherwise panic("librarySourcePath: fileIndex {fileIndex} is below project.librarySourcePaths.count()")

		if path.filename() == fileName 'itIsTheFile'
			return path
		end 'itIsTheFile'
	end 'eachFile'

	panic("librarySourcePath: no library source is named '{fileName}' — `StdlibLoader.loadLibrarySources` loads the whole of `stdlib/` and `runtime/` before anything reads them, and refuses a root that contributes nothing")
end 'librarySourcePath'

export function runtimeSourcePath(project Project, fileName String) returns FilePath
	for fileIndex in 0 upto project.librarySourcePaths.count() 'eachFile'
		let path = try project.librarySourcePaths.get(fileIndex) otherwise panic("runtimeSourcePath: fileIndex {fileIndex} is below project.librarySourcePaths.count()")

		if path.filename() == fileName and isRuntimeSourceFile(project, path: path) 'itIsTheTierFile'
			return path
		end 'itIsTheTierFile'
	end 'eachFile'

	panic("runtimeSourcePath: no `runtime/` source is named '{fileName}' — `StdlibLoader.loadLibrarySources` loads the whole of `runtime/` before anything reads it")
end 'runtimeSourcePath'

// The two answers `classifyLibraryReach` splits one walk into. One record because they partition one
// question over one roster, and separate derivations could come to disagree about which names each holds.
type LibraryReachSplit
	export let unreachable as FunctionNameSet
	export let unreachedRuntimeTier as FunctionNameSet

	static function create(unreachable FunctionNameSet, unreachedRuntimeTier FunctionNameSet) returns LibraryReachSplit
		return Self{unreachable: unreachable, unreachedRuntimeTier: unreachedRuntimeTier}
	end 'create'
end 'LibraryReachSplit'

// Split the library roster by what this compile REACHES, filing an unreached name under the question its
// tier can answer.
//
// ⛔⛔ **BUILT AND REACHED ARE THE SAME QUESTION FOR `stdlib/` AND TWO DIFFERENT ONES FOR `runtime/`.**
// `unreachable` means *no body was built for this, do not expect one*, and that is what every reader of
// the set acts on. For a runtime file it is false: its bodies are built unconditionally
// (`Queries.buildsBodiesFor`), because no source file outside the tier can CALL a runtime entry — the
// compiler reaches one BY NAME — so a walk over source call edges could never earn one its body. Filing a
// tier name `unreachable` would therefore empty a body the back end may still call.
// `UnusedExportCheck` exempts the same cone in the same terms and for the same reason.
//
// ⭐ **WHAT THE WALK DOES HOLD EVIDENCE ABOUT IS WHETHER THE PROGRAM REACHES THE TIER**, because the call
// the compiler mints is an ordinary `MaxonOp.call` in the caller's body long before this runs, and the
// tier's bodies are in the merged module too — so the answer is closed over runtime→runtime edges like any
// other. That is `unreachedRuntimeTier`, and its readers act on *"dead-function elimination will sweep
// this"* rather than on *"no body exists"*.
//
// ⛔⛔ **AN UNREACHED TIER NAME MUST REST ON POSITIVE EVIDENCE, BECAUSE THE TWO ERRORS ARE NOT ALIKE.**
// Omitting a name costs the program bytes; filing one whose body then survives leaves that body reading a
// `.data` word or a host chunk nothing laid out, which is a link failure. So every edge this walk does not
// model must resolve to "reached" — which is what the over-approximating `reachable` set already delivers.
//
// ⚠ **NEITHER SET KEEPS ANYTHING IN THE PROGRAM.** Dead-function elimination roots its own walk
// (`DeadFunctionElimination.seedRoots`) and reads both only to CHECK them
// (`requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead`), so a runtime entry nothing calls is still swept there and a
// program that reaches no runtime family carries none of it.
//
// The short-circuit caller passes an EMPTY `reachable`, which is precisely what it has concluded: no op in
// user code and no root crosses into library source — tier names included, since they are on the same
// roster the scan tests against — so nothing in it is reached.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND THE REACH ANSWER HAS A SECOND HALF THE WALK CANNOT PRODUCE — `roster.compilerReached`.** A tier
// body the compiler reaches through a call ONE OF ITS OWN INSTALLERS mints is reached by no edge in the
// Maxon module, so it is declared rather than discovered; `runtimeTierFileIsCompilerCalled` is where that
// roster and its staleness argument live. It is read HERE and not folded into `reachable` because it must
// answer on the short-circuit path too, where there is no walk to fold it into.
function classifyLibraryReach(roster LibraryFunctionRoster, reachable FunctionNameSet) returns LibraryReachSplit
	let unreachable = FunctionNameSet.create()
	let unreachedRuntimeTier = FunctionNameSet.create()

	for name in roster.names 'classify'
		if reachable.contains(name) or roster.compilerReached.contains(name) 'reached'
			continue
		end 'reached'

		if roster.runtimeTier.contains(name) 'theTier'
			unreachedRuntimeTier.insert(name)
			continue
		end 'theTier'

		unreachable.insert(name)
	end 'classify'

	return LibraryReachSplit.create(unreachable, unreachedRuntimeTier: unreachedRuntimeTier)
end 'classifyLibraryReach'

// The reachable-from-`main` closure over the merged Maxon module, as a set of function NAMES.
// The Maxon-tier twin of `DeadFunctionElimination.markCalleeOf`: the FOUR ops that name a callee
// are edges — `call`, `tryCall`, `functionRef` (a TAKEN ADDRESS is a static edge even though the
// call consuming it is indirect) and `asyncCall`. Every other variant names no static callee.
//
// A callee that is not a module function — an as-yet-uninstalled `__gt_*`/`__clock_*` runtime
// helper, which is added only during/after lowering — is simply not queued, exactly as
// `DeadFunctionElimination.markLiveFunction` handles the same case.
//
// ⛔⛔ **DIRECT `call`/`funcAddr` EDGES ARE NOT THE WHOLE OF STDLIB REACHABILITY: A STDLIB FUNCTION CAN
// SIT BEHIND A WITNESS DISPATCH.** An interface method implementation that prints reaches
// `stdlib/Print.maxon`'s `print` only through `__witness_<Type>.<Iface>`, a DATA edge, and missing it is a
// `requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead` panic on a correct program (`specs/interface-dispatch.md`'s
// `dispatch-void-method`).
//
// ⇒ **THE WITNESS EDGE IS CREDITED AT THE CONFORMER THIS WALK REACHES** — `WitnessCredit.owner`, which
// `WitnessEvidenceLedger.noteFunction` records for every reached function of a type.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE CONFORMER KEY RESTS ON TWO PREMISES, NOT ONE, AND ONLY THE FIRST IS SELF-EVIDENT.**
//
//   • **P1 — a conformer whose values are built by a CALL implies a reached function OF it.** This is E3076,
//     and it is enforced: `Thing{tag: 1}` written outside `Thing` is *"type 'Thing' can only be constructed
//     from within its own methods"*. ⚠ **IT COVERS ONLY THAT KIND OF CONFORMER, AND AN ENUM IS NOT ONE.** An
//     enum case is a TAG LITERAL — no function of the enum is reached for the value to exist, and the table
//     is minted at the widening all the same. An enum or union that declares `implements` is therefore
//     credited by the seed instead, per NAMED interface, exactly as the six builtin conformers are
//     (`enumConformersByInterface`). A generic INSTANCE is the remaining value kind built by no call, and it
//     is still REFUSED at the widening (`Box with ExitCode` into a `Printer` place is E3005), so it owes
//     nothing. ⇒ P1 holds for the conformers E3076 governs, and the seed stands in for the ones it does not.
//
//   • **P2 — a BUILTIN conformer's requirement impls are never stdlib SOURCE. IT DOES NOT HOLD, so it is
//     not a premise at all; the seed below stands in its place.** The conformer key structurally CANNOT
//     credit a builtin: no `String.<method>` or `int.<method>` module function is ever reached, since those
//     calls lower to `__str_*`/inline ops. Builtin conformers really do reach tables, and not only through
//     a container's key: `type Keyed uses T where T is Hashable` instantiated at `String` and at `int` builds `__witness_String.Hashable` and
//     `__witness_int.Hashable`. That is harmless only for an impl `BuiltinConformanceRuntime` SYNTHESIZES.
//     ⇒ **Where a LISTED module declares `extension <builtin> implements <Iface>` with a corpus body, this
//     key misses it.** Those conformers are carried by `conformersMintedWithoutTheirOwnMethods`, whose
//     membership rule and loudness argument live at that function.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND THIS IS WHY A RESIDUAL HERE IS TOLERABLE: AN UNDER-APPROXIMATION IS LOUD, ALWAYS.** The same
// walk decides both what is BUILT (`growStdlibBodyDemand`) and what is filed `unreachable`, so the two can
// never disagree in the silent direction — a function this walk misses is unbuilt AND in that set, and
// `requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead` panics the moment elimination reaches it through the reloc. The
// SILENT failure — filed reachable, body never built, an empty function the program calls — is a different
// mistake, and this key cannot make it. Treat a panic from that guard as
// this premise list having acquired a third entry, and add it here.
//
// ⚠ **A NARROWER GATE EXISTS AND IS NAMED RATHER THAN DENIED — it is not taken, and the reason is not
// that it is unsound.** `ensureWitnessTable`'s `interfaceName` argument is ALWAYS statically known: a
// declared interface-typed parameter, a declared interface RETURN, a `where T is I` constraint, or the
// container key/element's `Hashable`/`Equatable`. Gating on the PAIR — a reached conformer `T` AND an
// interface the program can statically name — would drop the owner credit's over-approximation (a
// `FilePath` program crediting impls it never boxes) to nothing. Taking it re-introduces predicting the
// CALL SITES, and its gain is no emitted bytes; and the `extends` case
// needs care (a `Base`-typed place against `T implements Derived` builds `__witness_T.Base`, so the filter
// must walk `extends` rather than test set membership).
//
// ⭐ The name index is the shared, CHECKED one (`FunctionNameIndex`). A last-wins, unchecked resolution
// would walk ONE of two same-named bodies and credit the callees of whichever it picked; a walk that
// silently under-approximates reachability must not rest on a tie-break nobody stated.
//
// ⚠ **THIS SITE RUNS BEFORE `installManagedGlobalInit`, SO IT CANNOT MEET A REAL DUPLICATE** — a
// `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` declaration of `__module_init`/`__maxon_global_cleanup` against the compiler's own
// has no compiler side yet. The refusal is not lost, it is simply made once: `PassPipeline.run`'s index sees
// the identical pair one screen below, and `DeadFunctionElimination.markCompilerOwnedRoot` catches the case
// with no pair to count. See `FunctionNameIndex`, which is where what each site can see is written down.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE ROOT SET IS `main` PLUS EVERY FUNCTION A TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZER CALLS, and the second half is
// not a widening — it is the same reachability with an edge no BODY carries.** `__module_init` makes those
// calls, and it is synthesized after this walk and called by the entry STUB, so it is invisible here in
// both directions. `DeadFunctionElimination.seedRoots` roots it unconditionally (its kind 2), so leaving
// the edge out is not conservative: the two derivations disagree, `lowerMaxonToStd` lowers no body for a
// stdlib factory named only by an initializer, and the elimination walk reaches the empty function it
// left: `var sep = FilePath.separator()` would be a `requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead` panic on a
// correct program (`specs/top-level-factory-globals.md`'s `stdlib-factory-reached-from-the-initializer`).
function reachableMaxonFunctionNames(module MaxonModule, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, stdlib FunctionNameSet, initCallees NameRoster, ledger WitnessEvidenceLedger) returns FunctionNameSet throws CompileError
	let byName = (try FunctionNameIndex.create(module.functions, project: project)).byName

	let reached = FixedBoolArray.create(module.funcCount())
	let worklist = FuncIndexArray.create(0)
	let names = FunctionNameSet.create()

	markOneReachable(project.entryFunctionName, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)

	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY `test` IS A ROOT HERE TOO, AND IT HAS TO BE, BECAUSE THE TWO WALKS ARE CHECKED AGAINST EACH
	// OTHER.** `DeadFunctionElimination.seedRoots` roots tests over the STD module; this walk roots them over
	// the MAXON one, and `requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead` PANICS when the second walk reaches a stdlib
	// body the first declared unreachable. A test that calls `print` or an `Expect` matcher is exactly that
	// shape — nothing reaches it from `main` — so a root missing here is a compiler abort on the first test
	// that calls the library, not a lost prune.
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunction'
		if isTestFunctionName(func.name) or not stdlib.contains(func.name) 'aRoot'
			markOneReachable(func.name, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		end 'aRoot'
	end 'eachFunction'

	for callee in initCallees 'eachInitializerRoot'
		markOneReachable(callee, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	end 'eachInitializerRoot'

	// ⭐⭐ **A CORPUS SLOT READ FROM A USER FUNCTION NOTHING CALLS IS STILL BUILT, SO ITS INITIALIZER'S
	// CALLEES ARE SEEDED FROM EVERY USER BODY AND NOT ONLY FROM THE REACHED ONES.** This asymmetry is
	// not this walk's invention — it is `DeadGlobalElimination.liveGlobalLabels`', stated in its own header:
	// *"A USER function is still credited whether or not anything calls it … but a stdlib function
	// `LibraryFacts.unreachable` names is skipped"*. That is the rule that decides whether the slot EXISTS,
	// so it is the rule that decides whether `__module_init` builds a record calling these bodies — and a
	// root missing under it is `requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead`, not a lost prune. Matching it exactly is
	// what keeps the two derivations from disagreeing; the reached-code half is `markCorpusSlotInitCallees`,
	// which covers the stdlib bodies this seed deliberately does not.
	seedCorpusSlotInitCalleesOfUserCode(module, signatures: signatures, stdlib: stdlib, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **A WITNESS IMPL IS CREDITED FOR A PAIR — A CONFORMER THE WALK HAS EVIDENCE OF, AND AN INTERFACE
	// THE REACHED CODE CAN NAME.** Each half asked ALONE over-credits: the conformer key credits every
	// conformance a reached type DECLARES (evidence about the type, none about the interface), and the corpus
	// seed credits every conformance the roster conformers declare (evidence about the interface's existence,
	// none about which one). Neither is wrong; together they are a cross product, and it is the CROSS that is
	// exact — `ensureWitnessTable` is called with a conformer AND an interfaceName, and its `interfaceName` is
	// always statically known.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE COST OF ASKING THEM SEPARATELY:** a program whose only constraint is `Hashable` would seed
	// `int.hash`, then the conformer key would see a REACHED `int.*` and credit every other `int`/`float` impl
	// — including `Stringable`'s `return "{self}"`, whose float arm drags `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s decimal
	// bignum cone — so the image carries `.rdata` panic literals for bodies the program cannot call.
	let creditedServices = CreditedServiceSet.create()

	while true 'seedRounds'
		drainReachableWorklist(module, project: project, signatures: signatures, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names, creditedServices: creditedServices, ledger: ledger, stdlib: stdlib)

		for impl in ledger.takeLiveImpls() 'eachLiveImpl'
			markOneReachable(impl, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		end 'eachLiveImpl'

		if worklist.count() == 0 'nothingLeftToDrain'
			break
		end 'nothingLeftToDrain'
	end 'seedRounds'

	return names
end 'reachableMaxonFunctionNames'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE CONFORMERS THE CONFORMER KEY STRUCTURALLY CANNOT REACH — the seed's membership rule, in one
// place, because the omission of a name from it is the whole defect this list exists to repair.**
// `WitnessCredit.owner` credits a type's requirement impls once the walk reaches ANY function OF that
// type, which is exact for a type E3076 governs (P1). It is EMPTY for a type the COMPILER MINTS VALUES OF: a
// `String` and a `Character` are born from literals and every operation on one lowers to `__str_*`, and an
// `Array` is born from an array literal or `Array.create()` and every operation on one lowers to
// `__managed_*`. So no reached function is ever qualified by one
// of these names and the key has nothing to fire on.
//
// ⚠ **MEMBERSHIP IS TWO CLAUSES AND BOTH ARE NARROWING, so "it is a builtin" is neither of them.**
//
//   • **The compiler mints its values without a call of its own** — as above. `Map` is a builtin container
//     and is deliberately ABSENT on this clause: a dictionary literal lowers to `Map.create` + `Map.upsert`
//     (`Parser.emitContainerLiteralCreate`), ordinary reached calls, so the conformer key fires for it.
//     `FixedArray` is absent for the same reason: `create`, `count`, `get` and `set` are `stdlib/FixedArray.maxon`'s,
//     so any program holding a fixed array reaches a `FixedArray.<method>`.
//   • **A LISTED module declares its conformance**, so a witness slot can name a CORPUS body. This is what
//     keeps `Set` off the list although it satisfies the first clause: its impls are
//     `BuiltinConformanceRuntime`'s synthesized ones, and a synthesized body is not stdlib source, so there
//     is nothing `unreachable` could hold and nothing to seed.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`int`, `float` AND `bool` MEET THE SECOND CLAUSE THROUGH `stdlib/PrimitiveExtensions.maxon`, WHOSE
// `extension int implements Hashable, Equatable, Comparable, Stringable, Cloneable` IS THE LISTED
// DECLARING MODULE IT NAMES.** `undefinedImplNames` therefore does not synthesize
// `int.hash`/`int.equals`/`int.compare` (or their `float`/`bool` siblings), and a witness slot's `funcAbs64`
// reloc names the CORPUS body instead — a DATA edge this Maxon-tier walk cannot see.
//
// ⚠ **THE THREE PRIMITIVE ENTRIES ARE THE BARE NAMES AND NEED NO `arrayRecordBaseName` TREATMENT**, because a
// primitive's spelling cannot be contested: `int`/`float`/`bool` are KEYWORDS, so no user `type int` exists
// to shadow the corpus declaration into `__int`. That is the same uncontestability `String` and `Character`
// rest on (`TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName`); only the array name moves.
//
// ⭐ **AN OMISSION HERE IS LOUD, ALWAYS, WHICH IS WHY A ROSTER IS TOLERABLE WHERE A PREDICTION WOULD NOT BE.**
// This walk decides both what is BUILT and what is filed `unreachable`, so a conformer missing from this list
// is a function `DeadFunctionElimination.requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead` PANICS on BY NAME the moment a
// witness slot reaches it.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE ARRAY ENTRY IS THE CORPUS DECLARATION'S *COMPILED* NAME AND NOT THE BARE ONE.** This
// seed names the qualifier a corpus body is registered under, and under a contest that is the mint
// (`__Array.hash`, `Parser.shadowRenamedTokens`): the bare `Array` there is the USER's own container, which
// declares no witness impl and so seeds nothing — leaving exactly the omission the paragraph above says
// PANICS BY NAME. `arrayRecordBaseName` is the corpus declaration as the stdlib spells it, which is what
// this walk reads. The other two names are uncontestable (`TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName`), so
// only this one moves.
function conformersMintedWithoutTheirOwnMethods(signatures ProgramSignatures) returns NameRoster
	let names = NameRoster.create(0)
	names.push(StringConformerName)
	names.push(CharacterConformerName)
	names.push(signatures.arrayRecordBaseName())
	names.push(IntConformerName)
	names.push(FloatConformerName)
	names.push(BoolConformerName)
	return names
end 'conformersMintedWithoutTheirOwnMethods'

// ⚠ **AN ENUM CONFORMER BELONGS TO THE SAME CLASS AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT LISTED ABOVE.** Membership of that
// list is a property of the COMPILER — those six names are fixed, so crossing them with every named interface
// is a constant — while which enums declare which interface is a property of the PROGRAM. Crossing the two
// growing sets would be quadratic in a fixpoint, so the enums are INDEXED by the interface a newly named interface is
// crossed with, and the inner work stays one row lookup.
typealias EnumConformersByInterface = Map with (ByteArray, NameRoster)

// ⭐ **THE ENUMS AND UNIONS WHOSE WITNESS SLOTS A NAMED INTERFACE OWES A CREDIT TO** — see P1 at
// `classifyLibraryReach` for why a conformer that no call builds values of needs one at all.
//
// Transitively closed through `extends`, because a `Base`-typed place against an enum declaring `Derived`
// builds `__witness_<Enum>.Base` and it is `Base`'s slots that are reached.
//
// ⚠ **BUILT ONCE PER WALK, OUTSIDE THE FIXPOINT**, for `conformersMintedWithoutTheirOwnMethods`' reason one
// declaration up. An interface with no witness slots is left out rather than carried empty: the credit marks
// one name per slot, so a slotless row could never mark anything — which is what keeps the corpus's several
// hundred `implements Error` enums out of this map entirely.
function enumConformersByInterface(project Project) returns EnumConformersByInterface
	let byInterface = EnumConformersByInterface.create()
	let declared = declaredConformanceIndex(project)

	for (conformerName, declaredInterfaces) in declared 'eachDeclaredConformer'
		if not project.enumTypes.contains(conformerName) 'itsValuesComeFromACall'
			continue
		end 'itsValuesComeFromACall'

		let reached = InterfaceNameSet.create()
		collectDeclaredInterfaceClosure(project.interfaces, declaredNames: declaredInterfaces, into: reached)

		for interfaceName in reached 'eachReachedInterface'
			recordEnumWitnessCredit(byInterface, project: project, interfaceName: interfaceName, enumName: conformerName)
		end 'eachReachedInterface'
	end 'eachDeclaredConformer'

	return byInterface
end 'enumConformersByInterface'

function recordEnumWitnessCredit(byInterface EnumConformersByInterface, project Project, interfaceName ByteArray, enumName ByteArray)
	let iface = try project.interfaces.entries.get(interfaceName) otherwise return

	if interfaceWitnessSlots(project.interfaces, iface: iface).count() == 0 'noSlotToCredit'
		return
	end 'noSlotToCredit'

	let conformers = try byInterface.get(interfaceName) otherwise NameRoster.create(0)
	conformers.push(enumName)
	byInterface.upsert(interfaceName, value: conformers)
end 'recordEnumWitnessCredit'

function drainReachableWorklist(module MaxonModule, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, byName FuncIndexByName, reached FixedBoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet, creditedServices CreditedServiceSet, ledger WitnessEvidenceLedger, stdlib FunctionNameSet)
	while worklist.count() > 0 'walk'
		let idx = try worklist.pop() otherwise panic("drainReachableWorklist: pop from a non-empty worklist")
		let func = module.getFunc(idx)
		markMaxonCallees(module, signatures: signatures, func: func, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names, creditedServices: creditedServices)
		ledger.noteFunction(func, module: module, project: project, signatures: signatures, stdlib: stdlib)
	end 'walk'
end 'drainReachableWorklist'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **A BUILTIN CONFORMER'S REQUIREMENT IMPLS ARE STDLIB SOURCE, SO THIS WALK MUST SEED THEM.**
// `stdlib/String.maxon` and `stdlib/Character.maxon` are LISTED modules, and each DEFINES the very
// `hash`/`equals`/`compare` a witness slot names. `BuiltinConformanceRuntime` synthesizes an impl only for
// a name the module does not already define (`undefinedImplNames`), so the witness slot's `funcAbs64` reloc
// names a CORPUS body.
//
// ⭐ **WHY THE CONFORMER KEY CANNOT REACH THESE, AND SO WHY A SEED IS NEEDED AT ALL.**
// `WitnessCredit.owner` credits a type's requirement impls once the walk reaches ANY function OF that
// type — sound for a user type, because E3076 makes a value of it impossible without one. A `String` value
// is born from a LITERAL and every operation on it lowers to `__str_*`, so **no `String.<method>` module
// function is ever reached** and the key has nothing to fire on. The table is still built, because
// `isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance` says these two conform with no `implements` at all.
//
// ⛔⛔ **AN UNCONDITIONAL SEED BREAKS THE RUNTIME FLOOR FOR EVERY PROGRAM IN THE LANGUAGE.** With the cone
// listed those five names ARE stdlib names, so a root list that seeds them unconditionally makes
// `anyNameReachesStdlib` answer TRUE for a program whose whole text is `return 0`: the
// allocation-free short-circuit never fires, the precise walk runs on every compile, and it reaches
// `Character.hash` — whose conformer key then credits `Character.clone`/`Character.init`, bodies that build
// the managed record `Character{managed: …}`. `scanRuntimeUsage` turns the heap on, the image is laid down
// with the allocator's `.data` slots in it, and the `static-variables/data-section-*` pins then read their
// `var` at a displaced offset — against the compile's own invariant, *"a `return 42` program
// allocates nothing, so it gets no allocator, no `.data` slots and no leak check"*.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **SO THE SEED IS GATED ON THE QUESTION IT IS THE ANSWER TO: CAN THE REACHED CODE NAME A WITNESS
// TABLE?** Every table in the emitted image is registered by `LowerMaxonToStd.ensureWitnessTable`, and it has
// exactly TWO routes in. This asks for the evidence each route needs, of REACHED code only — never for a
// prediction of which call sites will fire:
//
//   • **A `where T is I` CONSTRAINT witness** (`materializeWitnessArg`), carried by every call to a method of
//     a `where`-constrained type. Its evidence is that method's own DECLARATION, and the reached method is
//     exactly the one whose call materialized the table.
//   • **An EXISTENTIAL WIDENING at a call argument or a return** (`witnessForExistentialValue`). Same shape:
//     the widening happens at the CALLER, but it happens because the CALLEE declares an interface-typed
//     parameter or return — and a callee that is called is a callee this walk reaches.
//
// ⚠ **A THIRD ROUTE IS POSSIBLE AND THE CORPUS HAS NONE, AND IT IS THE ONE WHOSE EVIDENCE COULD ONLY BE AN
// OP: a COMPILER-OWNED runtime callee that threads a CONTAINER's key witnesses as ordinary arguments.**
// Every such callee's corpus counterpart is a declared generic method, so its witnesses are a DECLARATION the
// first route already covers. A new compiler-owned witness-carrying callee needs that route back, in
// `markMaxonCallees`, because nothing declares such a body's parameters.
//
// ⇒ **EVERY ROUTE'S EVIDENCE IS A REACHED DECLARATION, so the demand is discovered BY the
// drain, and the drain reruns until it credits no new impl** — see `reachableMaxonFunctionNames`. The seed stays an
// OVER-APPROXIMATION in the CONFORMER (a program that widens only an `int`, or keys a `Set` by one, still
// credits both byte records), and that is the safe direction by this walk's own rule: *"an OVER-approximation
// only ever removes names from `unreachable`, and every consumer of that set treats absence as 'compile it
// normally'."* The narrower gate — reading the receiver instance's element type off the value columns, as
// the lowering itself does — is available and is not taken: it re-derives lowering's conformer computation
// at the Maxon tier, and an UNDER-approximation there is an empty body the back end calls.
//
// ⚠⚠ **BOTH SIDES OF THE SHORT-CIRCUIT MUST ASK THIS SEED QUESTION.** `userCodeReachesStdlib`'s header
// warns that a root kind which is *"a marking pass"* must not be added to one side only: a seed only this
// side knows leaves a program whose user code names no stdlib function taking the short-circuit, filing
// every stdlib name `unreachable` INCLUDING these, and `requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead` panicking on
// the witness slot. So the two sides ask ONE
// PREDICATE — `functionDeclaresAWitnessPosition` — and the short-circuit's exactness argument carries it: if
// user code names no stdlib callee then NO stdlib function is reachable, so the reached set is a subset of
// user code, and a user function is the only place a demand can be.
//
// ⭐ **THIS HALF IS THE DECLARATION-BORNE PAIR**, asked of the CALLEE rather than of the call site, because
// the callee is what this walk holds and a caller's demand implies it:
//
//   • An interface-typed PARAMETER or RETURN: `appendExistentialWitnessArgs` materializes a table for a
//     concrete argument crossing into the parameter, and `lowerReturn` for one crossing into the return.
//   • A store into an interface-typed FIELD: the store is the widening site and names no callee, so its
//     evidence is the function's own `IrFunction.existentialFieldStores`, which the parser records beside the
//     `existentialWitness` op it emits.
//   • A `where` CONSTRAINT the method carries: `appendWitnessArgs` materializes one table per constraint at
//     every call to it. Asked through `witnessConstraintsOfMethod` — `ProgramSignatures.witnessParamCountForCallee`'s
//     own source — so the list this gate tests and the list that pass fills are one answer, and a conditional
//     TYPE EXTENSION's extra constraints (which no `structWhereConstraints` read would see) are covered by
//     construction.
// ⭐⭐ **AND IT ANSWERS WITH THE INTERFACE NAMES, NOT WITH A BOOL.** A yes/no seeds EVERY method of EVERY
// conformance each roster conformer declares. That is free for `String`/`Character`/`Array`, whose
// conformances are `Hashable`/`Equatable`/`Comparable` — bodies with no literals. **It is not free for
// `int`/`float`/`bool`**: their `Stringable` impl is `return "{self}"`, and a `float`'s interpolation drags
// `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s entire decimal bignum cone (`__bigMulSmall`, `__bigShiftLeft`,
// `__float_bitsFromText`, …) behind it.
//
// ⛔ A yes/no would grow the image of a program that names only `Hashable` by every `.rdata` panic literal
// those bodies register, none of which its code can reach: `DeadFunctionElimination` drops the BODIES, and
// nothing drops the literals a lowered body registered.
//
// ⇒ **The seed is keyed on the interfaces the reached code can actually NAME**, which is the narrower
// gate this walk's own header describes and declines: *“`ensureWitnessTable`'s `interfaceName` argument is
// ALWAYS statically known”*. The over-approximation that remains is the CONFORMER (a `Hashable` program
// still credits `int.hash` and `String.hash` alike), which is the cheap half — those are the bodies with no
// literals.
//
// ⚠ **UNDER-APPROXIMATING HERE IS A PANIC, NOT A SILENT WRONG ANSWER**, and `WitnessEvidenceLedger.noteNamedInterface`
// is what keeps it exact: a name discovered by a LATER function credits the seeds and the mentioned enums at
// that moment, so a witness position reached only through a seeded body is still covered.
function collectWitnessPositionInterfaces(func IrFunction, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, funcSignatures FuncSignatureMap, into NameRoster, listed FunctionScopedSet)
	for paramType in func.maxonParamTypes 'eachParam'
		if maxonTypeTag(paramType) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'existentialParam'
			noteWitnessPositionInterface(paramType, project: project, into: into, listed: listed)
		end 'existentialParam'
	end 'eachParam'

	match func.maxonReturnType 'returnPosition'
		void then break
		value(inner) then noteWitnessPositionInterfaceIfExistential(inner, project: project, into: into, listed: listed)
	end 'returnPosition'

	for constraint in witnessConstraintsOfReachedFunction(func, signatures: signatures, funcSignatures: funcSignatures) 'eachConstraint'
		noteInterfaceName(constraint.interfaceName, into: into, listed: listed)
	end 'eachConstraint'

	for store in func.existentialFieldStores 'eachFieldStore'
		noteInterfaceName(store.interfaceName, into: into, listed: listed)
	end 'eachFieldStore'
end 'collectWitnessPositionInterfaces'

// The interface an `interfaceRef` position names, filed once. The ids of a MERGED module's signature types
// are project-global (`mergeArtifact` remaps them), so the project's interner is the one they live in.
function noteWitnessPositionInterface(t MaxonType, project Project, into NameRoster, listed FunctionScopedSet)
	noteInterfaceName(interfaceNameOfType(t, typeNames: project.typeNames), into: into, listed: listed)
end 'noteWitnessPositionInterface'

// The RETURN arm's form: a return type is a `MaxonReturnType`, so the tag test lives past the `value` arm
// rather than in front of it — `maxonReturnTypeTag` panics on `void` by design.
function noteWitnessPositionInterfaceIfExistential(t MaxonType, project Project, into NameRoster, listed FunctionScopedSet)
	if maxonTypeTag(t) != ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'notAnExistential'
		return
	end 'notAnExistential'

	noteWitnessPositionInterface(t, project: project, into: into, listed: listed)
end 'noteWitnessPositionInterfaceIfExistential'

function noteInterfaceName(name ByteArray, into NameRoster, listed FunctionScopedSet)
	if listed.claim(name) 'notYetNamed'
		into.push(name)
	end 'notYetNamed'
end 'noteInterfaceName'

// ⭐ **THE SHORT-CIRCUIT SCAN'S HALF OF THE PAIR — THE SAME SOURCES, ASKED AS A YES/NO.**
// `userCodeDemandsAWitnessTable` walks EVERY user function before the demand walk runs and needs no names,
// so it must not pay for a set and an array per function. The two readers stay one answer by construction:
// every source below is one of the collector's, and neither may grow a source alone.
function functionDeclaresAWitnessPosition(func IrFunction, signatures ProgramSignatures, funcSignatures FuncSignatureMap) returns bool
	for paramType in func.maxonParamTypes 'eachParam'
		if maxonTypeTag(paramType) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'existentialParam'
			return true
		end 'existentialParam'
	end 'eachParam'

	if not maxonReturnTypeIsVoid(func.maxonReturnType) and maxonReturnTypeTag(func.maxonReturnType) == ValueTypeTag.interfaceRef 'existentialReturn'
		return true
	end 'existentialReturn'

	if func.existentialFieldStores.count() > 0 'storesIntoAnInterfaceField'
		return true
	end 'storesIntoAnInterfaceField'

	return witnessConstraintsOfReachedFunction(func, signatures: signatures, funcSignatures: funcSignatures).count() > 0
end 'functionDeclaresAWitnessPosition'

// The `where`-clause half of the collector above, kept whole because its two-branch read of the signature
// map is `functionDeclaresAWitnessPosition`'s own — see the paragraphs above.
function witnessConstraintsOfReachedFunction(func IrFunction, signatures ProgramSignatures, funcSignatures FuncSignatureMap) returns WhereConstraintArray
	// ⭐ **THE EXTENSION HALF IS READ OFF THE REGISTRATION NAME'S OWN SIGNATURE** — see
	// `FuncSignature.extensionConstraints`. A function with no recorded signature (a synthesized graph, a
	// witness adapter) has no registration name to read one off, so it falls back to the DECLARATION-SWEEP
	// clause, which cannot be wrong for a name no declaration wears. Over-answering here costs a walk;
	// under-answering skips a table the image needs.
	if let sig = try funcSignatures.get(func.name) 'declared'
		return signatures.witnessConstraintsOfMethod(func.name, extensionConstraints: sig.extensionConstraints)
	end 'declared'

	return signatures.witnessConstraintsOfMethod(func.name, extensionConstraints: signatures.extensionMethodConstraintsOf(func.name))
end 'witnessConstraintsOfReachedFunction'

module function markOneReachable(name ByteArray, byName FuncIndexByName, reached FixedBoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	let idx = try byName.get(name) otherwise 'notAModuleFunction'
		return
	end 'notAModuleFunction'

	let already = try reached.get(idx) otherwise panic("reachableMaxonFunctionNames: reached.get OOB at function index {idx}")

	if already 'alreadyReached'
		return
	end 'alreadyReached'

	try reached.set(idx, value: true) otherwise panic("reachableMaxonFunctionNames: reached.set OOB at function index {idx}")
	names.insert(name)
	worklist.push(idx)
end 'markOneReachable'

// Walk `block.bodyOps()` DIRECTLY — not `IrModule.liveOpIndices`, which allocates a fresh op-index array
// per function and answers the same question here, since a terminator names no callee. Walking `opRefs`
// is what makes this ORPHAN-PROOF, and that is load-bearing: `Parser.removeCurrentBlockOpAt` (the
// constant-slice fold) leaves a dropped `startIndex()`/`endIndex()` call holding its `module.ops` index and
// referenced by no block. ⇒ **A flat scan of `module.ops` at the Maxon tier is not equivalent to this
// walk**; `scanRuntimeUsage` walks `opRefs` for the same reason.
//
// ⚠ **A COMPILER-OWNED CALLEE THAT THREADS A WITNESS TABLE AS AN ORDINARY ARGUMENT IS ANSWERED HERE AND
// NOT IN A SECOND PASS**, riding along on the `maxonOpCalleeKind` CLASSIFICATION THIS LOOP ALREADY
// COMPUTES: a walk of its own would re-derive, on every compile, a callee name this one already holds. The
// corpus has no such callee — see `functionDeclaresAWitnessPosition`'s header for the two routes that
// remain.
function markMaxonCallees(module MaxonModule, signatures ProgramSignatures, func IrFunction, byName FuncIndexByName, reached FixedBoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet, creditedServices CreditedServiceSet)
	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("markMaxonCallees: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")

		for opIdx in block.bodyOps() 'eachOp'
			let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("markMaxonCallees: opRef {opIdx} not in module.ops")
			markMaxonCalleeEdge(op, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
			markCorpusSlotInitCallees(op, signatures: signatures, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
			markServiceBehaviourHandlers(op, signatures: signatures, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names, creditedServices: creditedServices)
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'eachBlock'
end 'markMaxonCallees'

// The static reachability edge one `MaxonOp` names, if any — asked of the DIALECT'S OWN classifier, which
// is the single place the op set answers "does this variant bind a callee name, and which operand is it?".
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT IS NOT A SECOND COPY OF THAT TABLE, AND COST IS NO REASON TO MAKE IT ONE.** Re-deriving the
// classification here with its own band ranges costs nothing to avoid: `maxonOpCalleeKind` is a SCALAR enum
// and allocates no union box per ask. A copy is only a second answer — a variant appended to `MaxonOp` does
// fail to compile at both sites, but nothing makes the two sites classify it the SAME WAY, and an
// under-approximation here is not a compile error: it is `lowerMaxonToStd` skipping a body that is then
// reached, which is a `requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead` panic.
//
// A `functionValue` target is credited alongside a `direct` callee: a `functionRef`'s target is reachable
// code even though no `call` op names it. `indirectCall` calls through the code POINTER that `functionRef`
// already credited, and `maxonOpCalleeKind` answers `noCallee` for it.
module function markMaxonCalleeEdge(op MaxonOp, byName FuncIndexByName, reached FixedBoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	match maxonOpCalleeKind(op) 'kind'
		direct or
		functionValue then markOneReachable(maxonOpCalleeName(op), byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
		noCallee then break
	end 'kind'
end 'markMaxonCalleeEdge'

// ⭐⭐ **A `spawn` REACHES EVERY HANDLER ITS SERVICE'S `__behave` DISPATCHES.** `<T>.__behave` is built after
// this walk (`installServiceBehaviours`), and `DeadFunctionElimination` reaches it through the `funcAddr` the
// `spawn` lowers to — so its calls are credited here, at the op that makes it live, from the roster it is
// built from (`serviceBehaviourHandlerCallees`). Its other callees are runtime entries and synthesized drops, never
// stdlib source.
//
// ⚡ Credited once per TYPE: the spawn sites and the messages of one service both grow with the program, and
// their product would not.
function markServiceBehaviourHandlers(op MaxonOp, signatures ProgramSignatures, byName FuncIndexByName, reached FixedBoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet, creditedServices CreditedServiceSet)
	if not maxonOpIsServiceSpawn(op) 'startsNoService'
		return
	end 'startsNoService'

	let typeName = maxonOpSpawnedServiceType(op)

	if creditedServices.contains(typeName) 'alreadyCredited'
		return
	end 'alreadyCredited'

	for handler in serviceBehaviourHandlerCallees(signatures, typeName: typeName) 'eachHandler'
		markOneReachable(handler, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	end 'eachHandler'

	creditedServices.insert(typeName)
end 'markServiceBehaviourHandlers'

function witnessOwnerOf(funcName immutable ByteArray) returns immutable ByteArray throws AbsentError
	match splitQualifiedName(funcName) 'owner'
		notQualified then throw AbsentError.absent
		parts(typeName, _) then return typeName
	end 'owner'
end 'witnessOwnerOf'

function ownerWitnessImplNames(typeName ByteArray, seedConformers NameRoster, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns NameRoster
	let impls = NameRoster.create(0)

	if seedConformers.contains(typeName) 'theSeedOwnsThisConformer'
		return impls
	end 'theSeedOwnsThisConformer'

	let conformedTo = try declaredConformanceIndex(project).get(typeName) otherwise 'declaresNoConformance'
		return impls
	end 'declaresNoConformance'

	for interfaceName in conformedTo 'eachInterface'
		for impl in witnessImplNamesOf(typeName, interfaceName: interfaceName, project: project, signatures: signatures) 'eachImpl'
			impls.push(impl)
		end 'eachImpl'
	end 'eachInterface'

	return impls
end 'ownerWitnessImplNames'

// Credit every requirement implementation `typeName`'s witness tables can name. A type that declares no
// conformance has no table and nothing to credit, which is almost every type in almost every program.
//
// ⚠ The index is `declaredConformanceIndex`'s, not a second walk of `project.conformances`: that memo is
// reset in lockstep with the store it derives from (`Queries.resetMergeTargets`), so it cannot answer this
// walk from a previous merge round's declarations.
// ⚠ **IT TAKES BOTH COORDINATES — the conformer AND the interface — which is `ensureWitnessTable`'s own
// argument list.** The conformer alone answers *"everything this type promises"*, while a table is built
// for an interface the reached code can NAME, and it is the pair that decides which slots exist.
//
// `typeConformsTo` is the gate rather than a bare membership test because it walks `extends` and admits
// an INTRINSIC conformance — the two ways a slot can exist without the type's own declaration naming this
// interface.
function witnessImplNamesOf(typeName ByteArray, interfaceName ByteArray, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns NameRoster
	let impls = NameRoster.create(0)

	if not typeConformsTo(declaredConformanceIndex(project), interfaces: project.interfaces, typeName: typeName, interfaceName: interfaceName, discovered: signatures.discoveredConformerOf(typeName)) 'thisPairHasNoTable'
		return impls
	end 'thisPairHasNoTable'

	let iface = try project.interfaces.entries.get(interfaceName) otherwise 'noSuchInterface'
		// A witness position naming an interface the program does not declare is E3017's / E3011's, reported
		// one pass later; a reachability walk has nothing to add and nothing to credit.
		return impls
	end 'noSuchInterface'

	// The TRANSITIVE requirement set, `ensureWitnessTable`'s own list — `iface.methods` alone would miss
	// every `extends`-inherited slot, which is a slot like any other. It is also what covers the `extends`
	// case the narrower gate has to get right: a `Base`-typed place against a `T implements Derived` builds
	// `__witness_T.Base`, whose slots are BASE's, and those are what this names.
	for slot in interfaceWitnessSlots(project.interfaces, iface: iface) 'eachSlot'
		impls.push(qualifiedMethodName(typeName, methodName: slot.method.name))
	end 'eachSlot'

	return impls
end 'witnessImplNamesOf'

function constraintWitnessImplNames(instance GenericInstanceId, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns NameRoster
	let impls = NameRoster.create(0)

	for constraint in signatures.structWhereConstraints(signatures.genericInstanceBaseName(instance)) 'eachConstraint'
		let conformer = signatures.conformerTypeArgName(instance, position: constraint.typeParamIndex).toByteArray()

		for impl in witnessImplNamesOf(conformer, interfaceName: constraint.interfaceName, project: project, signatures: signatures) 'eachImpl'
			impls.push(impl)
		end 'eachImpl'
	end 'eachConstraint'

	return impls
end 'constraintWitnessImplNames'

// Stdlib's own bodies are skipped for `userCodeReachesStdlib`'s reason and for `liveGlobalLabels`': an access
// inside an unreachable stdlib body decides nothing, because that body reaches no back end and its slot is
// not kept for it.
function seedCorpusSlotInitCalleesOfUserCode(module MaxonModule, signatures ProgramSignatures, stdlib FunctionNameSet, byName FuncIndexByName, reached FixedBoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		if stdlib.contains(func.name) 'skipStdlibBody'
			continue
		end 'skipStdlibBody'

		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("seedCorpusSlotInitCalleesOfUserCode: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")

			for opIdx in block.bodyOps() 'eachOp'
				let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("seedCorpusSlotInitCalleesOfUserCode: opRef {opIdx} not in module.ops")
				markCorpusSlotInitCallees(op, signatures: signatures, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
			end 'eachOp'
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'seedCorpusSlotInitCalleesOfUserCode'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE OTHER EDGE THIS WALK CANNOT SEE — A CORPUS SLOT'S INITIALIZER — CREDITED AT THE READ THAT
// KEEPS THE SLOT.** `installManagedGlobalInit` builds `__module_init` AFTER this walk, so the calls a
// global's initializer makes exist in no op the walk can read. What the walk CAN see is the `globalAddr` on
// that slot — which is precisely what decides the slot survives at all (`contributesStorage` keeps a corpus
// slot on liveness alone) — so the edge is credited there. Exact in both directions rather than
// approximated: keeping the slot is running the initializer, and dropping it is not.
//
// ⛔ **ROOTING THEM BESIDE `main`, AS `globalInitCallees` DOES FOR THE PROGRAM'S OWN DECLARATIONS, IS
// WRONG FOR THE CORPUS'.** See `TopLevelDecl.declaredByTheCorpus` for the two symptoms — an allocator and
// an orphan `.rdata` blob in every program in the language.
//
// ⚠ **OVER-CREDITING IS THE SAFE DIRECTION AND THIS DOES IT ONCE:** a read the program never executes
// still credits the callees, whose bodies are then lowered and dropped by dead-function elimination. A
// MISSING root is a `requireUnreachableLibraryStayedDead` panic.
function markCorpusSlotInitCallees(op MaxonOp, signatures ProgramSignatures, byName FuncIndexByName, reached FixedBoolArray, worklist FuncIndexArray, names FunctionNameSet)
	if not maxonOpNamesAGlobalLabel(op) 'namesNoSlot'
		return
	end 'namesNoSlot'

	for callee in signatures.initCalleesOfCorpusSlot(maxonOpGlobalLabelName(op)) 'eachCallee'
		markOneReachable(callee, byName: byName, reached: reached, worklist: worklist, names: names)
	end 'eachCallee'
end 'markCorpusSlotInitCallees'

// True iff any live op in USER code names a STDLIB callee, or a TOP-LEVEL INITIALIZER does — the
// allocation-free pre-scan that lets a program which never touches stdlib skip the reachability walk and
// the substrate fixpoint entirely. It asks the same dialect classifier `markMaxonCalleeEdge` does
// (`maxonOpCalleeKind`), and differs from it only in what it does with a hit: it runs on every compile, so
// it allocates nothing (no map, no queue) — it only asks "is one present?". The one exception is a program that
// spawns a service, whose handler roster `anyServiceHandlerReachesStdlib` builds once.
//
// ⚠ **IT ANSWERS OVER THE SAME LISTS THE PRECISE WALK ROOTS FROM — those shared lists are the guarantee,
// not a matching root SET**, and the difference is worth stating because the two are easy to confuse.
// The precise walk credits every callee a reached op names; this asks only "is this name a stdlib name?".
// Those agree EXACTLY, and here is why: a USER callee's own body is scanned by the loop below whatever it
// goes on to call, a STDLIB callee's name is its registration name and so is always in `stdlib`, and an
// undeclared one is refused (E3004) before it can reach either walk. An under-answer here would file ALL
// of stdlib unreachable for a program that reaches it — the direction that empties a body the back end
// then calls — so a new ROOT KIND owes that argument again rather than inheriting it.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE WITNESS-IMPL SEED IS SUCH A ROOT KIND.** Rooted in the precise walk alone, a program that took
// the short-circuit would file `String.hash` unreachable while its witness table still named it. It is a
// MARKING on both sides, driven by the pair of predicates this scan asks below and the drain asks of reached
// code — see `functionDeclaresAWitnessPosition`, which carries the argument in full.
function userCodeReachesStdlib(module MaxonModule, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, stdlib FunctionNameSet, initCallees NameRoster) returns bool
	// ⭐ **A ROOT THE PRECISE WALK SEEDS IS A CROSSING NO BODY CARRIES, WHICH IS WHY IT CANNOT BE FOUND BY THE
	// OP SCAN BELOW.** `var sep = FilePath.separator()` reaches stdlib without any function of this module
	// naming it — the call belongs to `__module_init`, which does not exist yet. Missing it would answer "all
	// of stdlib is unreachable" for a program that reaches it, the direction that skips a body the back end
	// then calls.
	if anyNameReachesStdlib(initCallees, stdlib: stdlib) 'rootedInStdlib'
		return true
	end 'rootedInStdlib'

	// ⭐⭐ **AND A WITNESS TABLE IS THE OTHER SUCH CROSSING: its slots reach `String.hash`/`Character.hash`/
	// `Array.hash` through an `.rdata` RELOCATION, which is an op nowhere.** So this asks the pair of demand
	// predicates the drain asks of reached code, over USER code — exact for the same reason the stdlib-callee
	// scan below is: with no user edge into stdlib, the reached set IS a subset of user code. Gated on the
	// corpus actually DECLARING one of the seed's conformances, so an unlisted declaring module leaves this
	// scan — and every program's answer — exactly as it was.
	if corpusDeclaresASeededConformance(project, signatures: signatures) and userCodeDemandsAWitnessTable(module, project: project, signatures: signatures, stdlib: stdlib) 'threadsOne'
		return true
	end 'threadsOne'

	// ⭐ **AND A SERVICE IS THE THIRD: `<T>.__behave` calls every handler, and it is built after this scan.**
	// A user handler's body is scanned below like any other, so what this answers for is a handler that IS
	// stdlib source. See `anyServiceHandlerReachesStdlib` for why it is asked of the roster and not per `spawn`.
	if signatures.spawnsAnyService() and anyServiceHandlerReachesStdlib(signatures, stdlib: stdlib) 'aHandlerIsStdlib'
		return true
	end 'aHandlerIsStdlib'

	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		// ⭐ SKIP STDLIB's OWN BODIES. Stdlib is internally connected (`Clock.elapsedMs` calls
		// `Clock.nowMs`), so scanning its bodies would find an intra-stdlib edge on EVERY compile and
		// defeat this short-circuit. The exact question is "does USER code cross INTO stdlib?", and the
		// first edge into stdlib on any path from `main` is made by a USER caller — `main` itself is never
		// stdlib — so scanning only user bodies is the exact condition. (A stdlib-only reference is
		// unreachable-from-`main` anyway, so a false negative here is impossible; a false positive would
		// merely trigger the exact full walk.)
		//
		// It reads the set being BUILT rather than `isLibraryFunction`, which reads the PUBLISHED one:
		// this runs inside the derivation, before `project.libraryFacts` holds anything.
		if stdlib.contains(func.name) 'skipStdlibBody'
			continue
		end 'skipStdlibBody'

		// Direct `block.bodyOps()` walk — allocation-free, and safe for the same reason `markMaxonCallees`
		// and `scanRuntimeUsage` use it at this tier (pre-pipeline: no orphan ops; callees are body ops).
		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("userCodeReachesStdlib: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")

			for opIdx in block.bodyOps() 'eachOp'
				let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("userCodeReachesStdlib: opRef {opIdx} not in module.ops")

				// ⚠ This short-circuit must answer TRUE for every edge a precise-walk seed roots, or the walk it
				// skips would have rooted a name this scan called unreachable.
				if opNamesStdlibCallee(op, stdlib: stdlib) or opReadsACorpusSlotRootedInStdlib(op, signatures: signatures, stdlib: stdlib) 'found'
					return true
				end 'found'
			end 'eachOp'
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'eachFunc'

	return false
end 'userCodeReachesStdlib'

// The short-circuit's half of the witness-table demand — the SAME predicate the drain asks of reached
// code (`functionDeclaresAWitnessPosition`), asked of USER code. It is a separate
// loop from the stdlib-callee scan above and deliberately so: that scan returns the moment it finds an edge
// into stdlib, so folding this in would make the answer depend on which of two independent facts a program
// happens to state first.
//
// It over-answers only by ignoring reachability — a user function nothing calls still counts — which is the
// direction that costs a walk rather than a wrong answer.
//
// ⚠ **This scan and `drainReachableWorklist` must ask the SAME predicates**, or the short-circuit would skip
// a walk that would have found a demand.
function userCodeDemandsAWitnessTable(module MaxonModule, project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures, stdlib FunctionNameSet) returns bool
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		// Stdlib's own declarations are skipped for `userCodeReachesStdlib`'s reason: a demand inside a stdlib
		// body is reached only through a user edge into stdlib, which that scan has already answered TRUE for.
		if stdlib.contains(func.name) 'skipStdlibBody'
			continue
		end 'skipStdlibBody'

		if functionDeclaresAWitnessPosition(func, signatures: signatures, funcSignatures: project.funcSignatures) 'widensOne'
			return true
		end 'widensOne'
	end 'eachFunc'

	return false
end 'userCodeDemandsAWitnessTable'

// Does the CORPUS declare a conformance on a conformer the seed exists for — i.e. is there a corpus body a
// witness slot could name at all? Without a declaring module listed, these conformers conform only
// INTRINSICALLY (`isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance`) and `BuiltinConformanceRuntime` synthesizes every impl, so
// no witness table can reach stdlib source and the demand question above must not disturb a single program's
// answer. Read off `declaredConformanceIndex` — the memo `witnessImplNamesOf` reads through — rather
// than off a second reading of `project.conformances`, so the gate and the marking cannot disagree about
// whether there is anything to credit.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT ASKS THE SEED'S OWN LIST, BECAUSE A SECOND SPELLING OF THAT LIST IS THE SHORT-CIRCUIT HALF OF
// EXACTLY THE DEFECT THE SEED REPAIRS.** `userCodeReachesStdlib`'s header records the obligation: a root
// kind added to the precise walk alone lets a program whose user code names no stdlib callee take the
// short-circuit and file the impl `unreachable` while its witness table still names it. A conformer added to
// the seed and not here would be that omission wearing a gate's clothes.
//
// ⚠ `stdlib/String.maxon` and `stdlib/Character.maxon` are listed UNCONDITIONALLY and each declares a
// conformance, so this gate is TRUE in every compile. The shared list keeps it right for the reason it is
// written — the seed and its gate are ONE roster — rather than by accident of which modules are listed.
function corpusDeclaresASeededConformance(project Project, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns bool
	let index = declaredConformanceIndex(project)

	for conformerName in conformersMintedWithoutTheirOwnMethods(signatures) 'eachSeededConformer'
		if index.contains(conformerName) 'declaresOne'
			return true
		end 'declaresOne'
	end 'eachSeededConformer'

	return false
end 'corpusDeclaresASeededConformance'

// Does any name on a list the precise walk marks reach stdlib? One function for every such list — the
// initializer roots and a service's handlers — so none of them can arrive with a subtly different test.
function anyNameReachesStdlib(names NameRoster, stdlib FunctionNameSet) returns bool
	for name in names 'eachName'
		if stdlib.contains(name) 'found'
			return true
		end 'found'
	end 'eachName'

	return false
end 'anyNameReachesStdlib'

// The short-circuit's half of `markServiceBehaviourHandlers`. Asked once over the ROSTER — every type any `spawn`
// starts, so a superset of what the precise walk credits, which only ever costs that walk — rather than at
// each `spawn`, whose handler roster would be rebuilt per site.
function anyServiceHandlerReachesStdlib(signatures ProgramSignatures, stdlib FunctionNameSet) returns bool
	for typeName in serviceTypeRoster(signatures) 'eachService'
		if anyNameReachesStdlib(serviceBehaviourHandlerCallees(signatures, typeName: typeName), stdlib: stdlib) 'found'
			return true
		end 'found'
	end 'eachService'

	return false
end 'anyServiceHandlerReachesStdlib'

// Whether one `MaxonOp` names a callee in `stdlib` — the SAME dialect classifier `markMaxonCalleeEdge`
// asks, over the same four callee-bearing variants.
//
// ⚠ It runs on every compile, so it must allocate nothing — and sharing the classifier costs nothing:
// `maxonOpCalleeKind` is a scalar enum, and the NAME is fetched only for the two kinds that carry one,
// i.e. only where this function reads a callee anyway.
function opNamesStdlibCallee(op MaxonOp, stdlib FunctionNameSet) returns bool
	return match maxonOpCalleeKind(op) 'kind'
		direct or
		functionValue gives stdlib.contains(maxonOpCalleeName(op))
		noCallee gives false
	end 'kind'
end 'opNamesStdlibCallee'

// ⭐⭐ **THE THIRD CROSSING NO BODY CARRIES: A USER READ OF A CORPUS SLOT WHOSE INITIALIZER CALLS
// STDLIB.** `type Cache / static let table = StrMap.create()` in a listed module reaches `Map.create` from
// `__module_init`, which `installManagedGlobalInit` has not built yet, so the op scan above sees only the
// `globalAddr` — and answering "all of stdlib is unreachable" there is the direction that skips a body the
// back end then calls.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS ASKED PER USER OP, NEVER OF THE WHOLE ARENA, AND THAT IS WHAT KEEPS THE SHORT-CIRCUIT.** A
// LISTED module's static is reachable only through an accessor of its own type — a stdlib call
// `opNamesStdlibCallee` already finds — so an arena-wide test would answer TRUE for every program in the
// language the day any listed module declares one. `stdlib/CharacterSet.maxon` declares eleven, so that is
// not hypothetical: it is the difference between `var flag = true` taking the allocation-free short-circuit
// and taking the full walk.
function opReadsACorpusSlotRootedInStdlib(op MaxonOp, signatures ProgramSignatures, stdlib FunctionNameSet) returns bool
	if not maxonOpNamesAGlobalLabel(op) 'namesNoSlot'
		return false
	end 'namesNoSlot'

	return anyCalleeIsIn(signatures.initCalleesOfCorpusSlot(maxonOpGlobalLabelName(op)), stdlib: stdlib)
end 'opReadsACorpusSlotRootedInStdlib'

// The loop the caller above keeps out of its one-line answer. It reads the set being BUILT rather than
// `isLibraryFunction`, for `userCodeReachesStdlib`'s stated reason: this runs inside the derivation that
// publishes it.
function anyCalleeIsIn(callees NameRoster, stdlib FunctionNameSet) returns bool
	for callee in callees 'eachCallee'
		if stdlib.contains(callee) 'crossesIntoStdlib'
			return true
		end 'crossesIntoStdlib'
	end 'eachCallee'

	return false
end 'anyCalleeIsIn'

// ⭐ **WHICH STDLIB FUNCTIONS REACH A HOST ENTRY THE TARGET LACKS, AND WHICH ENTRY EACH REACHES.**
// The fact a user's crossing call is blamed with, so the refusal names the stdlib function they wrote
// instead of a line inside `stdlib/`.
//
// ⭐ TRANSITIVE, because a stdlib module is written in LAYERS. `Clock.elapsedMs` names no runtime entry at
// all — it calls `Clock.nowMs`, which names `__gt_now_ns` — and a program calling `elapsedMs` must be
// refused at ITS call. So the requirement propagates BACKWARD along the stdlib call graph to a fixpoint:
// the seeds are the functions that name an entry directly, and a caller inherits its callee's.
//
// ⚠ **IT CARRIES THE ENTRY NAME, NOT A BIT**, which is why it is not `CsrGraph.reverseReachabilityMark`:
// `Clock` alone reaches TWO entries (`__gt_now_ns` for the monotonic clock, `__clock_now_unix_s` for the
// calendar), so a bit would leave the diagnostic naming whichever one it guessed — a wrong answer, not a
// cosmetic one. What PROPAGATES is still a scalar: a node inherits its callee's SEED INDEX, and the names
// are read off `seedEntries` once at the end.
//
// COST: O(stdlib functions + their ops + their intra-stdlib edges), bounded by the stdlib source LOADED
// and not by the user program, and computed only for a compile that actually calls into stdlib. Each node
// enters the worklist at most once and each reverse edge is walked once, so it stays linear in the loaded
// stdlib as that grows from today's one module to all of it.
//
// The UNREACHABLE stdlib functions are left out entirely: they are invisible to this compile, so their
// substrate is too. That is what keeps a program whose own source never names a host-only reader
// compiling for wasm.
function stdlibSubstrateEntries(module MaxonModule, stdlibNames NameRoster, unreachable FunctionNameSet, evidence WitnessEvidenceLedger, target Target) returns StdlibSubstrateEntryMap
	let nodeCount = stdlibNames.count()
	let nodeOf = StdlibNodeByName.create()

	for i in 0 upto nodeCount 'index'
		nodeOf.upsert(try stdlibNames.get(i) otherwise panic("stdlibSubstrateEntries: stdlibNames.get({i}) OOB below its own count()"), value: i as CsrIndex)
	end 'index'

	let state = SubstrateFixpoint.overNodes(nodeCount as CsrIndex, target: target)

	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		if unreachable.contains(func.name) 'notInTheProgram'
			continue
		end 'notInTheProgram'

		if let node = try nodeOf.get(func.name) 'stdlibFunction'
			for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
				let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("stdlibSubstrateEntries: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")

				for opIdx in block.bodyOps() 'eachOp'
					let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("stdlibSubstrateEntries: opRef {opIdx} not in module.ops")
					noteSubstrateEdge(op, callerNode: node, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
				end 'eachOp'
			end 'eachBlock'

			for impl in evidence.liveImplsOf(func.name) 'eachLiveImpl'
				noteSubstrateCallee(impl, callerNode: node, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
			end 'eachLiveImpl'
		end 'stdlibFunction'
	end 'eachFunc'

	state.propagateToCallers()

	let entries = StdlibSubstrateEntryMap.create()

	for i in 0 upto nodeCount 'publish'
		if state.requiresSubstrate(i as CsrIndex) 'needsIt'
			entries.upsert(try stdlibNames.get(i) otherwise panic("stdlibSubstrateEntries: stdlibNames.get({i}) OOB in publish"), value: state.entryOf(i as CsrIndex))
		end 'needsIt'
	end 'publish'

	return entries
end 'stdlibSubstrateEntries'

// The mutable state of the substrate fixpoint over the stdlib call graph's DENSE node space. It is one
// object rather than six threaded columns because the body scan and the propagation write all of it, and a
// helper that took them apart could be handed five of the six.
//
// ⭐ THE COLUMNS PROPAGATE SCALARS. A marked node stores an INDEX into `seedEntries`, never the entry name
// itself. The names are read once, after the fixpoint has settled.
type SubstrateFixpoint
	// Per node: does it (transitively) reach an entry the target lacks? Also the "already queued" test — a node is
	// marked exactly when it enters the worklist, so it enters at most once.
	var marked as FixedBoolArray

	// Per MARKED node: the index into `seedEntries` of the seed it inherits its entry from.
	var seedOf as FixedCsrColumn

	// The runtime entry each seed names, in the order the seeds were found.
	var seedEntries as NameRoster

	// Nodes whose callers still have to inherit from them. Drained by index, never popped, so a node's
	// position is stable while the array grows.
	var worklist as CsrColumn

	// The intra-stdlib call graph, REVERSED as it is collected (callee -> caller), so the propagation
	// walks from a function that names an entry to the functions that reach it.
	var edgeCallee as CsrColumn
	var edgeCaller as CsrColumn

	let target as Target

	static function overNodes(nodeCount CsrIndex, target Target) returns SubstrateFixpoint
		return Self{marked: FixedBoolArray.create(nodeCount), seedOf: FixedCsrColumn.create(nodeCount), seedEntries: NameRoster.create(0), worklist: CsrColumn.create(0), edgeCallee: CsrColumn.create(0), edgeCaller: CsrColumn.create(0), target: target}
	end 'overNodes'

	// `node` names `entry` directly — a SEED.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ ***"The first entry a body names is the one it is reported for: a body naming two is refused for
	// either, and both are equally true of it"* IS FALSE.**
	// The entry decides WHICH DIAGNOSTIC the crossing gets, not merely which name it quotes, so two
	// entries are two different claims with different advice. `SemanticCheck.substrateEntryIsPreferred` is the
	// one place that says which wins and why (rank, then the least entry name, so the answer never depends on
	// the order the walk met them in); here it is only obeyed. A preferred entry REPLACES the incumbent and
	// re-queues the node, so the callers that already inherited the other one are corrected.
	//
	// ⚠ The replacement is MONOTONE — preference is a strict total order over distinct entries, so a node's
	// entry only ever improves and a node is re-queued at most once per distinct entry; the fixpoint below
	// stays linear.
	//
	// ⚠ An entry the target provides seeds nothing. A node holds one entry, so a body reaching a provided
	// entry and a lacking one must record the lacking one, or its crossing passes the gate and the
	// program reaches a backend that cannot lower it. Per-target state is sound because `LibraryFacts`
	// is rebuilt for every compile and a `Project` has one target for its whole life.
	function seed(node CsrIndex, entry ByteArray)
		if not calleeNeedsFacilityTargetLacks(self.target, callee: entry) 'theTargetProvidesIt'
			return
		end 'theTargetProvidesIt'

		let already = try self.marked.get(node) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.seed: marked.get({node}) outside the node space")

		if already and not substrateEntryIsPreferred(entry, incumbent: self.entryOf(node)) 'keepTheIncumbent'
			return
		end 'keepTheIncumbent'

		self.mark(node, seedIndex: self.seedEntries.count() as CsrIndex)
		self.seedEntries.push(entry)
	end 'seed'

	// A call edge inside stdlib, recorded reversed for the propagation below.
	function callEdge(calleeNode CsrIndex, callerNode CsrIndex)
		self.edgeCallee.push(calleeNode)
		self.edgeCaller.push(callerNode)
	end 'callEdge'

	// Mark `node` as needing the substrate `seedIndex` names, and queue its callers for the same.
	function mark(node CsrIndex, seedIndex CsrIndex)
		try self.marked.set(node, value: true) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.mark: marked.set({node}) outside the node space")
		try self.seedOf.set(node, value: seedIndex) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.mark: seedOf.set({node}) outside the node space")
		self.worklist.push(node)
	end 'mark'

	// ⭐ THE FIXPOINT: every stdlib function that REACHES a seed over stdlib's own call edges inherits its
	// entry. A reverse BFS over the CSR adjacency — each node enters the worklist once (the marking IS the
	// visited test) and each edge is walked once, for O(nodes + edges).
	function propagateToCallers()
		let callersOf = buildCsr(self.edgeCallee, to: self.edgeCaller, nodeCount: self.marked.count() as CsrIndex)
		var head = 0 as CsrIndex

		while head < self.worklist.count() as CsrIndex 'drain'
			let node = try self.worklist.get(head) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.propagateToCallers: worklist.get({head}) below its own count()")
			head = head + 1
			let seedIndex = try self.seedOf.get(node) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.propagateToCallers: seedOf.get({node}) outside the node space")

			for k in 0 upto callersOf.degree(node) 'eachCaller'
				let caller = callersOf.neighbour(node, k: k)

				if self.inheritsFrom(caller, seedIndex: seedIndex) 'newlyRequiredOrOutranked'
					self.mark(caller, seedIndex: seedIndex)
				end 'newlyRequiredOrOutranked'
			end 'eachCaller'
		end 'drain'
	end 'propagateToCallers'

	// Should `caller` take `seedIndex` as its own — because it has no entry yet, or because this one
	// is PREFERRED to the entry it already inherited (`SemanticCheck.substrateEntryIsPreferred`)? An unmarked caller is
	// the ordinary case and answers before any name is read; the second question is asked only of a node that
	// is already marked, so the common propagation pays one bool test.
	//
	// ⚠ It is a question rather than an inlined `if` because the propagation and `seed` must ask the SAME one:
	// a seed that upgrades a node while its callers keep the weaker entry would leave the two halves of one
	// fixpoint disagreeing about which diagnostic a crossing earns.
	function inheritsFrom(caller CsrIndex, seedIndex CsrIndex) returns bool
		if not (try self.marked.get(caller) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.inheritsFrom: marked.get({caller}) outside the node space")) 'noEntryYet'
			return true
		end 'noEntryYet'

		return substrateEntryIsPreferred(try self.seedEntries.get(seedIndex) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.inheritsFrom: seed {seedIndex} does not exist"), incumbent: self.entryOf(caller))
	end 'inheritsFrom'

	function requiresSubstrate(node CsrIndex) returns bool
		return try self.marked.get(node) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.requiresSubstrate: marked.get({node}) outside the node space")
	end 'requiresSubstrate'

	// The runtime entry `node` reaches. Defined only for a node `requiresSubstrate` accepts — an unmarked
	// node's `seedOf` slot was never written, and the panics say so rather than returning slot 0's name.
	function entryOf(node CsrIndex) returns immutable ByteArray
		if not self.requiresSubstrate(node) 'unmarked'
			panic("SubstrateFixpoint.entryOf: node {node} needs no substrate")
		end 'unmarked'

		let seedIndex = try self.seedOf.get(node) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.entryOf: seedOf.get({node}) outside the node space")
		return try self.seedEntries.get(seedIndex) otherwise panic("SubstrateFixpoint.entryOf: node {node} names seed {seedIndex}, which does not exist")
	end 'entryOf'
end 'SubstrateFixpoint'

// One stdlib body op's contribution to the substrate fixpoint: it either NAMES a substrate runtime entry
// (a seed) or calls another stdlib function (a reverse edge). A callee can never be both — the parser
// reserves the `__` prefix for compiler-owned names, so no stdlib declaration can carry one.
//
// The band structure — and the band-append protection — is `markMaxonCalleeEdge`'s, the third member of
// that family in this file. Each asks a different question of the SAME four callee-naming variants, and
// each pays a different cost (this one runs only for a compile that calls into stdlib), which is why they
// are separate matches; a variant appended past `witnessTryDispatch` fails to compile in all three rather
// than being silently swallowed by any of them.
function noteSubstrateEdge(op MaxonOp, callerNode CsrIndex, nodeOf StdlibNodeByName, state SubstrateFixpoint)
	match op 'op'
		call(_, callee, _, _, _, _, _) then noteSubstrateCallee(callee, callerNode: callerNode, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
		literal or
		ret or
		retVoid or
		binOp or
		unaryOp or
		compare or
		condBranch or
		branch or
		globalAddr or
		loadIndirect or
		storeIndirect or
		stringLiteral then break
		tryCall(_, _, callee, _, _, _, _, _) then noteSubstrateCallee(callee, callerNode: callerNode, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
		throwError or
		propagateError or
		panicAbort then break
		functionRef(_, funcName, _) then noteSubstrateCallee(funcName, callerNode: callerNode, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
		indirectCall then break
		asyncCall(_, callee, _, _, _, _, _) then noteSubstrateCalleeAndEntry(callee, entry: GtSpawnName, callerNode: callerNode, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
		await or
		tryAwait or
		sizeofType or
		constArrayLiteral or
		switch or
		witnessDispatch or
		witnessTryDispatch then break

		// A panic names no callee at all, so it reaches no stdlib substrate.
		panicAbortValue then break

		// The error-flag encode names no callee at all, so it reaches no stdlib substrate.
		errorFlagEncode then break

		// The service and `async` ops are seeds as well as edges: a stdlib body is never blamed for its own
		// ops, so the entry each lowers to must reach the user's crossing through this fixpoint.
		serviceSpawn(_, _, _, _, callee, _, _, _, _, _, _) then noteSubstrateCalleeAndEntry(callee, entry: CownSpawnName, callerNode: callerNode, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)

		// ⭐ A SEND REACHES ITS MESSAGE, so it is an EDGE exactly as an `async` spawn is — the handler runs on
		// another green thread, but it is the same function and it reaches whatever that function reaches.
		serviceSend(_, _, callee, _, _, _, _, _) then noteSubstrateCalleeAndEntry(callee, entry: CownSendName, callerNode: callerNode, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)

		// A REPLY CELL names the message only to derive its releaser symbol; the `serviceSend` beside it is
		// the edge to that message, and noting it twice would credit one call graph edge to two ops.
		serviceReplyCell then noteSubstrateCallee(GtCellAllocName, callerNode: callerNode, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
		immortalStructRecord or
		immortalStringTable or
		covPoint then break

		// ⭐⭐ **A `__Raw` OP IS A SEED WITH NO CALLEE TO NAME IT, AND IT IS THE ONLY KIND THERE IS.** Every
		// other seed here is a CALL to a runtime entry, which the crossing can meet by name; a raw row has no
		// symbol between it and the machine, so without this arm a lane missing the facility reached
		// instruction selection with nothing said.
		rawIntrinsic(_, intrinsic, _, _) then noteSubstrateRawIntrinsic(intrinsic, callerNode: callerNode, state: state)

		// A witness table's impls are reached through the conformer, never through an edge this op names.
		existentialWitness or
		holdValue or
		holdEnd then break
	end 'op'
end 'noteSubstrateEdge'

// A `__Raw` row a library body spells: it SEEDS the fixpoint with the row's own mangled spelling where the
// row names a host facility, so the crossing into that body carries the need out to user code.
//
// ⚠ A row whose facility is `none` is pure code generation on every supported lane and seeds nothing — a
// seed would make every crossing into every tier body carry an entry no target can lack, which is a whole
// fixpoint's worth of work for a refusal that can never fire.
function noteSubstrateRawIntrinsic(intrinsic RawIntrinsic, callerNode CsrIndex, state SubstrateFixpoint)
	match rawHostFacilityOf(intrinsic) 'facility'
		none then return
		required then state.seed(callerNode, entry: rawIntrinsicCalleeName(intrinsic))
	end 'facility'
end 'noteSubstrateRawIntrinsic'

// Classify ONE callee named by a stdlib body: a runtime entry that needs a HOST FACILITY SEEDS the
// fixpoint, another stdlib function becomes a reverse edge, and anything else — a user callback, or a
// target-neutral runtime helper — carries no substrate requirement.
//
// ⭐ `calleeHostFacility` is asked, never restated: it is the ONE place that knows which runtime entries
// need a facility at all, and a second list here would be that fact written down twice. Whether the
// target provides it is `SubstrateFixpoint.seed`'s question.
function noteSubstrateCallee(callee ByteArray, callerNode CsrIndex, nodeOf StdlibNodeByName, state SubstrateFixpoint)
	if calleeHasHostFacility(callee) 'substrateEntry'
		state.seed(callerNode, entry: callee)
		return
	end 'substrateEntry'

	if let calleeNode = try nodeOf.get(callee) 'stdlibSibling'
		state.callEdge(calleeNode, callerNode: callerNode)
	end 'stdlibSibling'
end 'noteSubstrateCallee'

function noteSubstrateCalleeAndEntry(callee ByteArray, entry ByteArray, callerNode CsrIndex, nodeOf StdlibNodeByName, state SubstrateFixpoint)
	noteSubstrateCallee(entry, callerNode: callerNode, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
	noteSubstrateCallee(callee, callerNode: callerNode, nodeOf: nodeOf, state: state)
end 'noteSubstrateCalleeAndEntry'
